{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_8","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '888,1124p' docs/design/dao.jing.dht.md && cat src/jolt/dao/stream/ws/tcp.jolt\"","aggregated_output":"## 10. Today's code, and the slices that replace it\n\nWhat the tree holds on 2026-09-30, all of it replaced by this epic with no\ncompatibility path:\n\n<table>\n<tr>\n<th>\nToday\n</th>\n<th>\nFate\n</th>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<code>dao.jing.dht/IDhtNet</code>, <code>lookup</code>,\n<code>create-content-dht</code> (a waiting transport protocol; put replicates\nand get fetches synchronously)\n</td>\n<td>\nDeleted in S3, together with <code>dao.jing.dht.node</code>, which implements\nand calls them (Architect ruling on the S2 sign-off). S2's core does not use\nthem.\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<code>dao.jing.dht.node</code> (<code>create-node</code>,\n<code>create-content-dht-udp</code>; JVM only, own <code>DatagramSocket</code>\nand receiver thread, one Transit-JSON map per datagram, node id\n<code>sha256(host:port)</code>, claimed <code>:from</code> observed unvalidated,\nreplies matched by id alone, oversize dropped silently)\n</td>\n<td>\nDeleted in S3.\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<code>dao.jing.dht.kad</code>\n</td>\n<td>\nKept.\n</td>\n</tr>\n<tr>\n<td>\n<code>test/dao/jing/dht_test.cljc</code>,\n<code>test/dao/jing/dht/node_test.cljc</code>, the fake net in\n<code>test/yin/vm/linker_test.cljc</code>\n</td>\n<td>\nRewritten in S2 and S3.\n</td>\n</tr>\n</table>\n\n### Slice plan\n\nOrder: **S1 and S2 -> S3 -> S4 -> S5.** S1 and S2 own disjoint files and neither\nconsumes anything the other produces. No slice before S5 touches\n`yin/repl/*`.\n\n**Base64 seam.** The format is one: padded standard-alphabet Base64, strict.\nS2's codec is the existing `dao.jing/bytes->base64` and\n`dao.jing/base64->bytes`, frozen as its seam; S2 adds no codec and waits on\nnone. S1 creates `dao.stream.base64` for the datagram layer, byte-for-byte\nthe same format, and does not touch `dao.jing`. S3, which joins the two,\nrepoints `dao.jing`'s two functions onto `dao.stream.base64` and proves both\nagree on every test vector.\n\n**S1: raw datagram stream.** `dao.stream.datagram` (portable), the three\nhost seams, `dao.stream.base64`, `dao.stream.udp`'s `port-step!`.\nAcceptance:\n- Exact byte and observed-source round trips over loopback on JVM, Node and\n  Dart, through the writer handle and the traffic ring.\n- `bound`, `bind-failed`, `send-failed` and `closed` are observed as events;\n  no seam takes a function to invoke.\n- Hostname destination, bad Base64 and oversize are `invalid-value` with no\n  send; a closed socket is `closed`; inbound oversize is never deposited.\n- A slow reader observes `gap` on the traffic ring.\n- `test/dao/stream/udp_test.cljc` and every `dao.stream.remote` test pass\n  unchanged; host seam tests are rewritten.\n- Value-channel throughput is measured before and after and reported.\n\n**S2: DHT core on in-memory sockets.** `dao.jing.dht` state and step,\nlookup as state, pending writes and gets, the store handle, solo mode, the\nJVM facade, the `:jing/unacknowledged` completion in `dao.jing.content.step`.\nPeers are wired through ring buffers carrying raw datagram values; S2 needs\nno socket and no S1. It speaks the section 7 wire for single-datagram\nmessages and implements the whole cookie protocol of section 8 (issue, echo,\nverify, the gate, the size rule, padding, freshness) with the S2 stand-in\n`cookie-for`. Chunks are S3; the keyed MAC is S4. Acceptance:\n- An acknowledged write used, for every counted peer, the cookie that peer's\n  fresh reply carried; a store with a missing or wrong cookie causes no work.\n- The need-cookie reply never encodes to more than `first-contact-bytes`, and\n  a cookie-less datagram shorter than that reply is answered with silence.\n- Requests and answers are the exact `:jing/*` shapes; the unmodified content\n  clients drive a DHT.\n- With a warm table a write is acknowledged in the step that read it, after\n  exactly `ack-peers` counted sends, and the fact says `/sent`.\n- `put-bytes-fn` performs no lookup and no peer wait; the publish/readback\n  round completes against `:local`.\n- Each `/unacknowledged` reason of 4.3 is produced by a test, including cold\n  bootstrap reaching `too-few-peers` at the tick deadline and then\n  succeeding once peers are proven.\n- Solo mode composes no socket streams and holds no pending work.\n- A file-backed local verdict survives restart.\n- Time advances only by appended ticks; no test sleeps.\n- The JVM facade has one step owner and respects deadlines.\n- `dao.jing.md`'s backend list and status are updated.\n\n**S3: real sockets and chunks.** Depends on S1 and S2. Chunk records and\nthe move onto `dao.stream.datagram` sockets. Loopback only. Acceptance:\n- Two and three peers exchange small queries and multi-datagram segments\n  under injected loss and reordering.\n- The largest legal `:find` reply fits one datagram.\n- Oversize refuses before any send; malformed, duplicate and inconsistent\n  chunks stay within every bound.\n- A chunked write counts a peer only when every chunk answered `ok`.\n- **One transport-neutral chunk split/absorb utility is used by both\n  `dao.stream.udp` and the DHT**, each keeping its own wire fields and keys.\n  **Declared fallback:** if the two identities resist clean parameterization,\n  the DHT ships its own reassembly and `dao.stream.udp` is left untouched.\n  The slice report says which was taken and why.\n- Real covered-index nodes are measured against `max-message-bytes`.\n- JVM<->Node and JVM<->Dart pass the same wire tests.\n- `dao.jing.cbor.md` (Remote and DHT) is updated to this wire.\n- `dao.jing`'s Base64 functions are repointed onto `dao.stream.base64`.\n\n**S4: hardening, lookup repair, storage bound.** Depends on S3. The keyed\n`cookie-for` replacing the S2 stand-in, the chunk path under the gate, and\nevery bound. Acceptance:\n- A cookie cannot be computed without the secret; the S2 stand-in is gone.\n- A spoofed source elicits at most one reply, never larger than the datagram\n  it sent, and silence when the reply would be larger; it allocates nothing.\n  Tested across datagram sizes from 1 byte to the budget, chunks included.\n- A reply from an unexpected source is ignored; a claimed address never\n  enters the table.\n- A live next-nearest peer is tried after dead peers, and dead peers leave\n  the table.\n- Inbound storage at its bound refuses explicitly.\n- `publish?` false neither originates nor serves.\n- Non-loopback exposure is permitted only after this slice.\n\n**S5: REPL integration.** Depends on S4 and on the durable-store epic (spec,\nHEAD, lock, recovery). Acceptance:\n- Default `mem`; `dht:<dir>` is explicit; with no peers it is solo and opens\n  no socket.\n- Publishing requires its own flag, separate from peers, and the REPL states\n  what will be shared before it shares.\n- The REPL shows per publication whether it is acknowledged (sent to N\n  peers) or not and why; no round waits on the network.\n- Two processes with separate locked directories exchange content.\n- A reader given a manifest address hydrates and queries a remote index.\n\n### The plain Clojure path (S5, owner direction 2026-10-01)\n\nOwner, verbatim: \"plain clojure code should be able to query for code in the\ndht. the yin.repl should use the same path as the clojure repl via\nhost-functions\".\n\n`dao.space.dht` (CLJC; JVM, Node, Dart) is that path. It lives under\n`dao.space` because it joins a `dao.jing.dht` node to `dao.space.index` and\n`dao.space.query`; putting it under `dao.jing` would make the\npayload-agnostic well aware of index structure. It has no `yin.repl`\ndependency.\n\n- `(join opts)` composes a node over `:local` (a `dao.jing` store the\n  caller has opened, and locked if it is a directory) or `:dir`, which\n  `join` opens as the durable directory store `dao.space.store/open`:\n  exclusively, under its directory lock (OS locks on the JVM and Dart,\n  claim entries on Node, and the in-process registry), so a second owner in\n  this or another process is refused naming the directory; `close!`\n  releases it. The REPL's `file:<dir>` and `dht:<dir>` stores open the\n  same store, so a REPL and a plain node never share a directory. Options:\n  `:peers`\n  (none is solo: no socket, no secret), `:publish?` (default false, never\n  implied by peers), `:bind-host` (default `127.0.0.1`), `:bind-port`\n  (default 0), `:max-inbound-bytes` (default 64 MiB), `:bind!` (the host\n  datagram seam; each build's own by default). A node with peers mints its\n  root secret at join: 32 CSPRNG bytes, held only in the node value.\n- `(step node now)` is the node's only advance; `now` is its owner's\n  nondecreasing millisecond reading, appended as a tick. It answers\n  `[node events]` (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 4.3 and 5.5.3):\n  - `:bound`, `:bind-failed`;\n  - `:published`, the first report of an announced publication, once every\n    blob has an outcome: `:result` (`:acknowledged`, `:partial` or\n    `:unacknowledged`), `:blobs`, `:sent`, `:peers`, `:failed` (every blob\n    not sent, with its reason), `:repairing?`, and `:ended` when repair\n    will not run;\n  - `:republished`, the same shape, whenever that result changes after the\n    first report or its automatic repair ends;\n  - `:publication-unknown` (the facts were lost);\n  - `:loaded` and `:load-failed`, one per load, with its `:kind` and, on\n    failure, a `:reason` that is data.\n- `(store node)` is the node's byte-store handle; a put records the address\n  for the node's next publication and asks the DHT for nothing.\n  `(announce! node manifest)` closes the publication its puts made. `step`\n  paces the replicate requests below `max-pending-writes` through a bounded\n  backlog, so the node's own writes never draw `/busy`. `retry!` and\n  `cancel!` act on a live publication (`yin.vm.linker.dht.md` 5.5).\n- `(load node address {:kind k :walk f})` starts a staged load over any\n  walk; `(load-index node manifest)` is `load` with the covered-index walk,\n  which reads the index through the local store with\n  `dao.space.index/read-manifest`. `step` fetches each blob the walk names\n  missing with a `:jing/get` through `dao.jing.content.step`, until all\n  four covered indexes read back and cover the manifest's count. `forget`\n  clears a terminal load.\n- `(db node manifest)` is `dao.space.query/published-db` over the loaded\n  index (`restored-indexes`); `(q node manifest query & inputs)` is\n  `dao.space.query/q` over its current view.\n\n`yin.repl` composes its `dht:<dir>` store with `join` (over its locked\ndurable store), and its `dao.space.dht` host module answers `load-index`,\n`load-status` and `q` with these functions (`yin.repl.dao.space-index.md`,\n\"DHT store\").\n\nOut of this epic: pinning, garbage collection, availability repair and\nreconciliation sweeps, NAT meeting, a browser transport, latest-root\ndiscovery, authenticated node ids.\n\n**Loading code for evaluation** is the next epic, specified in\n[`yin.vm.linker.dht.md`](./yin.vm.linker.dht.md). It generalizes\n`load-index` into `dao.space.dht/load` (a staged load over any walk, with\nfailure reasons as data), adds a bounded replicate backlog so a round's\nmany row writes never report `/busy`, replaces the `:published` event's\n`:acknowledged?` with a result of `:acknowledged`, `:partial` or\n`:unacknowledged` that lists every blob not sent, retries a publication\nthat is not acknowledged automatically while the node is open (reported by\na `:republished` event), and makes the node's store the linker's content\nsource. It changes nothing in sections 2 to 9.\n\n## Relationship to the intake-pool observer\n\nThe DHT is a content-store backend, not a stream transport. The intake-pool\n(ns dao.stream.ws.tcp\n  \"Jolt-only TCP substrate under the native WebSocket host adapter (J6d,\n   plan v2 §3; docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §9.3): nonblocking\n   stream sockets over `jolt.socket.native`, never the `jolt.socket` java.net\n   shim, whose write path waits for the socket to drain.\n\n   Every descriptor made here is nonblocking and close-on-exec, and carries\n   SIGPIPE suppression (SO_NOSIGPIPE on macOS from `new-socket` and\n   `guard-accepted!`, MSG_NOSIGNAL per send on Linux), so a write to a peer\n   that has gone answers an error instead of ending the process. An accepted\n   descriptor is made nonblocking explicitly: Linux does not inherit the\n   listener's mode.\n\n   Nothing here waits. Connect answers `:connecting` on EINPROGRESS and\n   `connect-result` reads SO_ERROR once the descriptor is writable; recv and\n   send are one transfer each (EINTR retried) answering data, never throwing\n   for a socket condition. End of stream, reset and broken pipe are distinct\n   classes.\n\n   Names: `parse-address` accepts IPv4 and IPv6 literals and `localhost`\n   (mapped to 127.0.0.1) and never calls the resolver (plan v2 A6); any\n   other host is `{:error :host-name-unsupported}`.\"\n  (:require\n    [clojure.string :as str]\n    [jolt.ffi :as ffi]\n    [jolt.socket.native :as native]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Addresses (plan v2 A6)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- literal\n  \"{:family :ip} for the numeric literal `s`, the ip in its canonical\n   presentation form, or nil. inet_pton only; no resolver.\"\n  [s]\n  (some (fn [family]\n          (when-let [[sa _] (native/make-sockaddr family s 0)]\n            (try {:family family, :ip (native/sockaddr-ip sa)}\n                 (finally (ffi/free sa)))))\n        [native/af-inet native/af-inet6]))\n\n\n(defn- literal-shaped?\n  \"Whether `s` reads as an attempted address literal rather than a name:\n   only digits and dots, or any colon.\"\n  [s]\n  (boolean (or (str/includes? s \":\") (re-matches #\"[0-9.]+\" s))))\n\n\n(defn parse-address\n  \"`{:family :ip :port}` for `host` and `port`, or `{:error kind}` with kind\n   `:host-name-unsupported` (a name other than localhost), `:malformed-address`\n   (a literal inet_pton refuses) or `:invalid-port`. A bracketed IPv6 literal\n   (`[::1]`) is accepted. Never resolves: names are decidable here and now.\"\n  [host port]\n  (let [h (when (string? host)\n            (if (and (str/starts-with? host \"[\") (str/ends-with? host \"]\"))\n              (subs host 1 (dec (count host)))\n              host))]\n    (cond\n      (not (and (int? port) (<= 0 port 65535))) {:error :invalid-port}\n      (or (nil? h) (str/blank? h)) {:error :malformed-address}\n      (= \"localhost\" (str/lower-case h)) {:family native/af-inet, :ip \"127.0.0.1\", :port port}\n      :else (if-let [lit (literal h)]\n              (assoc lit :port port)\n              {:error (if (literal-shaped? h) :malformed-address :host-name-unsupported)}))))\n\n\n(defn- with-sockaddr\n  \"(f sa len) over a fresh sockaddr for a parsed address, freed after.\"\n  [{:keys [family ip port]} f]\n  (let [[sa len] (native/make-sockaddr family ip port)]\n    (try (f sa len) (finally (ffi/free sa)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Error classes\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn error-class\n  \"The class of socket error `e`: :reset, :broken-pipe, :refused,\n   :not-connected or :other. A reset and a broken pipe are both a peer that\n   has gone; they stay distinct for diagnostics.\"\n  [e]\n  (cond\n    (= e native/econnreset) :reset\n    (and native/epipe (= e native/epipe)) :broken-pipe\n    (= e native/econnrefused) :refused\n    (= e native/enotconn) :not-connected\n    :else :other))\n\n\n(defn- failure\n  [kind e]\n  {:error kind, :class (error-class e), :errno e, :message (native/error-message e)})\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Descriptors\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn close!\n  \"Close `fd` once. EINTR is not retried: on Linux the descriptor is released\n   whatever close answers, and a retry could close a reused number.\"\n  [fd]\n  (native/c-close fd)\n  nil)\n\n\n(defn shutdown!\n  \"shutdown(2) `fd` for :read, :write or :both; answers nil, ignoring\n   ENOTCONN (a peer that already went).\"\n  [fd how]\n  (native/c-shutdown fd (case how :read native/shut-rd :write native/shut-wr native/shut-rdwr))\n  nil)\n\n\n(defn- nonblocking!\n  \"O_NONBLOCK on `fd`; answers nil or the errno it failed with.\"\n  [fd]\n  (let [[r e] (native/set-blocking! fd false)]\n    (when (neg? r) e)))\n\n\n(defn- open-socket\n  \"A fresh nonblocking, close-on-exec stream socket of `family`, or a\n   failure map.\"\n  [family]\n  (let [fd (try (native/new-socket family) (catch Throwable _ nil))]\n    (if (nil? fd)\n      {:error :socket-failed}\n      (if-let [e (nonblocking! fd)]\n        (do (close! fd) (failure :socket-failed e))\n        {:fd fd}))))\n\n\n(defn local-port\n  \"The port `fd` is bound to (the kernel's pick after port 0), or -1.\"\n  [fd]\n  (native/local-port fd))\n\n\n(defn nonblocking?\n  \"Whether `fd` has O_NONBLOCK set, read back with F_GETFL.\"\n  [fd]\n  (let [[flags _] (native/c-fcntl fd 3 0)]\n    (and (not (neg? flags))\n         (pos? (bit-and flags (if (= native/os :macos) 0x4 0x800))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Listener\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn listen!\n  \"A nonblocking listener on the parsed `addr` (`parse-address`):\n   `{:fd :port}` with the actual port, or a failure map (`:error`\n   `:bind-failed`/`:listen-failed`/`:socket-failed`). The descriptor is\n   closed on failure.\"\n  ([addr] (listen! addr 128))\n  ([addr backlog]\n   (let [{:keys [fd] :as opened} (open-socket (:family addr))]\n     (if-not fd\n       opened\n       (let [_ (native/set-listener-reuse! fd)\n             [rb eb] (with-sockaddr addr (fn [sa len] (native/c-bind fd sa len)))]\n         (if (neg? rb)\n           (do (close! fd) (failure :bind-failed eb))\n           (let [[rl el] (native/c-listen fd backlog)]\n             (if (neg? rl)\n               (do (close! fd) (failure :listen-failed el))\n               {:fd fd, :port (local-port fd)}))))))))\n\n\n;; One accept(2) each; the test seam (redefined by the probe only).\n(defn accept-once\n  [lfd sa lenp]\n  (native/c-accept lfd sa lenp))\n\n\n(defn accept!\n  \"One nonblocking accept on listener `lfd`: `{:fd :peer}` for a connection,\n   explicitly nonblocking, close-on-exec, SIGPIPE-suppressed and\n   TCP_NODELAY; `:would-block` when none is pending; or a failure map\n   (`:error :accept-failed`). EINTR is retried.\"\n  [lfd]\n  (let [[sa lenp] (native/alloc-sockaddr)]\n    (try\n      (loop []\n        (let [[fd e] (accept-once lfd sa lenp)]\n          (cond\n            (not (neg? fd))\n            (if-let [e (nonblocking! fd)]\n              (do (close! fd) (failure :accept-failed e))\n              (do (native/guard-accepted! fd)\n                  (native/set-int-option! fd native/ipproto-tcp native/tcp-nodelay 1)\n                  {:fd fd\n                   :peer {:family (native/sockaddr-family sa)\n                          :ip (native/sockaddr-ip sa)\n                          :port (native/sockaddr-port sa)}}))\n            (native/eintr? e) (recur)\n            (native/eagain? e) :would-block\n            :else (failure :accept-failed e))))\n      (finally (ffi/free sa) (ffi/free lenp)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Dialer\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn connect!\n  \"Start a nonblocking connect to the parsed `addr`: `{:fd :state\n   :connected}`, `{:fd :state :connecting}` (EINPROGRESS: wait for the\n   descriptor to be writable, then `connect-result`), or a failure map\n   (`:error :connect-failed`), the descriptor closed. Never waits.\"\n  [addr]\n  (let [{:keys [fd] :as opened} (open-socket (:family addr))]\n    (if-not fd\n      opened\n      (let [[r e] (with-sockaddr addr (fn [sa len] (native/c-connect fd sa len)))]\n        (native/set-int-option! fd native/ipproto-tcp native/tcp-nodelay 1)\n        (cond\n          (not (neg? r)) {:fd fd, :state :connected}\n          ;; an interrupted connect carries on asynchronously, as EINPROGRESS\n          (or (native/connect-pending? e) (native/eintr? e)) {:fd fd, :state :connecting}\n          :else (do (close! fd) (failure :connect-failed e)))))))\n\n\n(defn connect-result\n  \"The outcome of a connect that answered `:connecting`, read once its\n   descriptor is writable: `:connected` or a failure map (`:error\n   :connect-failed`, from SO_ERROR). Does not close the descriptor.\"\n  [fd]\n  (let [e (native/pending-error fd)]\n    (cond\n      (zero? e) :connected\n      (neg? e) {:error :connect-failed, :class :other, :errno -1, :message \"SO_ERROR unreadable\"}\n      :else (failure :connect-failed e))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Transfers\n;; =============================================================================\n\n;; One system call each, answering [result errno]. The chunk functions call\n;; them through these vars, which is the test-only indirection the J6d probe\n;; redefines to script short, EAGAIN and failing transfers. There is no\n;; production fault mode.\n\n(defn recv-once\n  [fd buf n]\n  (native/c-recv fd buf n 0))\n\n\n(defn send-once\n  [fd buf n]\n  (native/c-send fd buf n native/msg-nosignal))\n\n\n(defn recv-chunk!\n  \"One recv of at most `cap` bytes into native `buf`: `{:n n}` (n > 0),\n   `:eof`, `:would-block`, or a failure map (`:error :recv-failed`, `:class`\n   :reset for ECONNRESET). EINTR is retried.\"\n  [fd buf cap]\n  (loop []\n    (let [[n e] (recv-once fd buf cap)]\n      (cond\n        (pos? n) {:n n}\n        (zero? n) :eof\n        (native/eintr? e) (recur)\n        (native/eagain? e) :would-block\n        :else (failure :recv-failed e)))))\n\n\n(defn send-chunk!\n  \"One send of `n` bytes from native `buf`: `{:n accepted}` (possibly short,\n   possibly 0), `:would-block`, or a failure map (`:error :send-failed`,\n   `:class` :broken-pipe or :reset for a peer that has gone). EINTR is\n   retried.\"\n  [fd buf n]\n  (loop []\n    (let [[r e] (send-once fd buf n)]\n      (cond\n        (not (neg? r)) {:n r}\n        (native/eintr? e) (recur)\n        (native/eagain? e) :would-block\n        :else (failure :send-failed e)))))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat src/jolt/dao/stream/ws/pump.jolt && rg --files | rg '(j0b|udp_probe|udp-probe|udp-probe|jolt.*udp|udp.*jolt|AGENTS.md\"'$)'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_9","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"cat src/jolt/dao/stream/ws/pump.jolt && rg --files | rg '(j0b|udp_probe|udp-probe|udp-probe|jolt.*udp|udp.*jolt|AGENTS.md\"'$)'\"'\"","aggregated_output":"(ns dao.stream.ws.pump\n  \"Jolt-only owner-thread pump under the native WebSocket host adapter (J6d;\n   J6 plan rulings A1-A5). One instance per `create` call: its registry of\n   descriptors, write queues, close records and deadlines live in that\n   instance and nowhere else, so two instances never share state.\n\n   Ownership (A1). Every entry runs on the thread that drives the instance;\n   nothing here waits, parks or polls a socket. A driver polls the `interest`\n   descriptors until the earlier of its cadence and `next-deadline`\n   (`jolt.io-poller/clock-ms`, nil when nothing is due), then hands the\n   `[[fd revents] ...]` it observed to `pump!`, `[]` on a timeout: every\n   call runs the due expiries. A composition that never pumps an instance\n   makes no progress, by design.\n\n   Bounded turn (A1). One `pump!` visits each ready descriptor at most once,\n   starting after the one visited last in the previous turn (round robin in\n   registration order). A connection visit attempts at most one write of\n   the head of its queue and reads at most one chunk (one reused 64 KiB\n   native buffer); a listener visit accepts at most `accept-batch`.\n\n   One ordered write path (A3). Each connection owns one FIFO of whole wire\n   segments; the head carries an offset and is never rewritten. `send!`\n   writes at once only into an empty FIFO and queues the unsent tail;\n   otherwise it appends. Protocol output (pongs, close frames) joins the\n   same FIFO at generation time; nothing has priority. `send!` answers nil\n   or `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}`, never false.\n   `queued-bytes` counts every byte handed over and not yet accepted by the\n   kernel, control frames included; 0 after the terminal.\n\n   Two connection modes. A :raw connection (an HTTP upgrade in progress, or\n   a plain byte stream) delivers `:on-data` chunks, `:on-eof` once and\n   `:on-error` once. `upgraded!` turns it into a :ws connection, which parses\n   its input one frame at a time through the injected `:protocol`\n   (`{:parser :parse-frame :encode-frame :close-payload :mask-key}`; the\n   parse contract is `(parse-frame state chunk offset)` =>\n   `[event-or-nil state' offset']` with `:message`, `:ping`, `:pong`,\n   `:close` and `:fail` events, a close-frame failure carrying `:close?\n   true`), answers each ping with one pong, delivers `:on-message`, and\n   reports exactly one `:on-closed` terminal.\n\n   Control budget (A4). At most `max-pongs` pending pongs per connection plus\n   one reserved close slot. When the pongs fill the budget the connection\n   stops parsing after that frame, leaves read interest and keeps the\n   unparsed rest of its chunk; when a drain frees a pong slot the rest is\n   parsed before any new read, and read interest returns once it is\n   consumed. The close slot never counts against the budget and is never\n   refused.\n\n   Close record (A5). `{:local nil|:queued|:sent, :peer nil|:received,\n   :peer-status nil|:absent|:invalid|code, :code, :reason, :initiator,\n   :deadline}`. The first of a local `close!` and the peer's close frame\n   starts the 5 s `close-deadline`; the first local request wins. The\n   terminal code: a completed handshake reports the local code when this\n   side initiated, else the peer's status (an empty close reports 1005, an\n   invalid one 1002); a peer close followed by end of stream before the\n   response left reports the peer's status; end of stream, reset or a\n   transfer failure without a peer close reports 1006; deadline expiry in\n   any state reports 1006. A parser `:fail` without `:close?` fails the\n   connection (RFC 6455 section 7.1.7) and opens no handshake: the record\n   gets `:failed? true`, the close frame takes the reserved slot, input is\n   read only to see end of stream and discarded, and once the frame is\n   written the terminal is the local code with cause `:fail`; the peer's\n   reply is never parsed. A `:fail` after a local `close!` keeps that\n   request's frame and code and ends the same way. 1005 and 1006 are never\n   put on the wire. A ping that arrives once a local close is queued gets no\n   pong: the pong would queue behind our close frame, RFC 6455 section 5.5.1\n   forbids only data frames after a close, and no Autobahn case exercises\n   it.\n\n   Listeners. Accepted sockets register with `:listener` and `:upgrading?`;\n   at `max-pending-upgrades` upgrading sockets (or `:max-connections` owned\n   ones) the listener leaves the interest set until a slot frees. An\n   upgrade not completed within its timeout is released and reported to\n   `:on-upgrade-expired`. `close-listener!` closes the listener descriptor;\n   `drained?` answers true once no connection it owns is left, and its\n   `:on-drained` is called once then. `lower-deadlines!` brings the close\n   deadline of every connection a listener owns to at most a given time.\n\n   Callbacks. Data callbacks (`:on-data`, `:on-message`) run inside the\n   visit. Terminals and other lifecycle reports run at the start or end of\n   a turn, never inside an entry the composition called; `next-deadline`\n   answers now while one is owed.\n\n   Native memory: the read and write buffers exist while the registry is\n   non-empty and are freed when its last descriptor leaves.\"\n  (:require\n    [dao.stream.ws.tcp :as tcp]\n    [jolt.ffi :as ffi]\n    [jolt.io-poller :as poller]\n    [jolt.socket.native :as native]))\n\n\n(def chunk-bytes\n  \"The read chunk and the largest single write attempt.\"\n  (* 64 1024))\n\n\n(def accept-batch\n  \"The most connections one listener visit accepts.\"\n  16)\n\n\n(def max-pongs\n  \"Pending pongs per connection before its input pauses (A4).\"\n  16)\n\n\n(def close-deadline-ms\n  \"From the first close event to forced teardown (A5).\"\n  5000)\n\n\n(def max-pending-upgrades\n  \"Default per-listener cap on accepted, not yet upgraded sockets (A4).\"\n  64)\n\n\n(def upgrade-timeout-ms\n  \"Default deadline for an upgrade, from accept or connect (A4).\"\n  10000)\n\n\n(def closed-outcome {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed})\n\n\n(def ^:private no-close\n  {:local nil, :peer nil, :peer-status nil, :code nil, :reason nil, :initiator nil,\n   :deadline nil})\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Instance\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn create\n  \"A fresh pump instance with an empty registry.\"\n  []\n  {::state (atom {:handles {}           ; fd -> handle, registered only\n                  :last-id -1           ; id of the handle visited last\n                  :next-id 0\n                  :notify []            ; thunks owed (terminals, lifecycle reports)\n                  :closed-listeners {}  ; id -> handle, closed and not yet reported drained\n                  :rbuf nil\n                  :wbuf nil})})\n\n\n(defn- st\n  [p]\n  (::state p))\n\n\n(defn- cell\n  [h]\n  @(::cell h))\n\n\n(defn- cell!\n  [h f & args]\n  (apply swap! (::cell h) f args))\n\n\n(defn- alloc-buffer\n  []\n  (let [b (ffi/alloc chunk-bytes)]\n    (when (ffi/null? b)\n      (throw (ex-info \"dao.stream.ws.pump native allocation failed\" {:bytes chunk-bytes})))\n    b))\n\n\n(defn- maybe-release!\n  \"Free the native buffers once the registry is empty.\"\n  [p]\n  (let [s (st p)\n        {:keys [handles rbuf wbuf]} @s]\n    (when (and rbuf (empty? handles))\n      (swap! s assoc :rbuf nil :wbuf nil)\n      (ffi/free rbuf)\n      (ffi/free wbuf))))\n\n\n(defn- owe!\n  \"Queue thunk `f` for the start or end of a turn.\"\n  [p f]\n  (swap! (st p) update :notify conj f)\n  nil)\n\n\n(defn- register!\n  [p fd fields]\n  (let [s (st p)]\n    (when-not (:rbuf @s)\n      (swap! s assoc :rbuf (alloc-buffer) :wbuf (alloc-buffer)))\n    (let [h {:id (:next-id @s), :fd fd, ::cell (atom fields)}]\n      (swap! s #(-> % (assoc-in [:handles fd] h) (update :next-id inc)))\n      h)))\n\n\n(defn- registered?\n  [p h]\n  (identical? h (get-in @(st p) [:handles (:fd h)])))\n\n\n(defn open?\n  [h]\n  (= :open (:status (cell h))))\n\n\n(defn- owned\n  \"The registered connections listener `lh` owns.\"\n  [p lh]\n  (filter #(= (:id lh) (:owner (cell %))) (vals (:handles @(st p)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Registration\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn listener!\n  \"Register listener `fd` (nonblocking, listening). Options: `:on-accept`\n   (each `{:fd :peer}`, inside the visit; the callee registers it with\n   `connection!` passing `:listener` and `:upgrading? true`, or closes it;\n   without one, accepted descriptors are closed), `:on-accept-error`,\n   `:on-drained`, `:max-pending-upgrades` (default 64), `:max-connections`\n   (default nil, unbounded), `:upgrade-timeout-ms` (default 10 s). Answers\n   the handle.\"\n  [p fd opts]\n  (register! p fd (merge {:max-pending-upgrades max-pending-upgrades\n                          :upgrade-timeout-ms upgrade-timeout-ms}\n                         (select-keys opts [:on-accept :on-accept-error :on-drained\n                                            :max-pending-upgrades :max-connections\n                                            :upgrade-timeout-ms])\n                         {:kind :listener, :status :open, :accepting? true})))\n\n\n(defn connection!\n  \"Register connection `fd` (nonblocking) in :raw mode. `:connecting? true`\n   marks a dial in progress: its first ready turn reads SO_ERROR and calls\n   `:on-connected` or reports `:on-error`. `:listener` names the owning\n   listener; `:upgrading? true` starts the upgrade deadline (the listener's\n   or `:upgrade-timeout-ms`) and counts against the listener's pending\n   cap. `:close-deadline-ms` overrides the 5 s close deadline. Callbacks: `:on-data`, `:on-eof`, `:on-error`, `:on-drained`,\n   `:on-upgrade-expired`, and for :ws mode `:on-message`, `:on-closed`.\n   Answers the handle.\"\n  [p fd {:keys [connecting? listener upgrading?] :as opts}]\n  (let [timeout (or (:upgrade-timeout-ms opts)\n                    (when listener (:upgrade-timeout-ms (cell listener)))\n                    upgrade-timeout-ms)]\n    (register! p fd (merge (select-keys opts [:on-connected :on-data :on-eof :on-error\n                                              :on-drained :on-upgrade-expired\n                                              :on-message :on-closed])\n                           {:kind (if connecting? :connecting :connection)\n                            :mode :raw\n                            :status :open, :pending [], :queued 0, :reading? true\n                            :owner (:id listener)\n                            :upgrading? (boolean upgrading?)\n                            :upgrade-deadline (when upgrading? (+ (poller/clock-ms) timeout))\n                            :pongs 0, :remainder nil\n                            :close-deadline-ms (or (:close-deadline-ms opts) close-deadline-ms)\n                            :close no-close}))))\n\n\n(defn upgraded!\n  \"Turn :raw connection `h` into a :ws connection parsing through\n   `protocol` (`{:parser :parse-frame :encode-frame :close-payload\n   :mask-key}`); `leftover` (bytes after the upgrade head, or nil) is parsed\n   before any new read. Frees its pending-upgrade slot.\"\n  [_p h protocol leftover]\n  (cell! h assoc\n         :mode :ws, :protocol protocol, :parser (:parser protocol)\n         :upgrading? false, :upgrade-deadline nil\n         :remainder (when (and leftover (pos? (alength leftover)))\n                      {:chunk leftover, :offset 0}))\n  nil)\n\n\n(defn set-accepting!\n  \"Pause (false) or resume (true) accepting on listener `h`.\"\n  [_p h accepting?]\n  (cell! h assoc :accepting? (boolean accepting?))\n  nil)\n\n\n(defn set-reading!\n  \"Stop (false) or resume (true) reading connection `h`.\"\n  [_p h reading?]\n  (cell! h assoc :reading? (boolean reading?))\n  nil)\n\n\n(defn set-deadline!\n  \"Call `(f)` once on the first turn at or after monotonic `at-ms`\n   (`jolt.io-poller/clock-ms`). Replaces any earlier such deadline of `h`.\"\n  [_p h at-ms f]\n  (cell! h assoc :deadline at-ms :on-deadline f)\n  nil)\n\n\n(defn clear-deadline!\n  [_p h]\n  (cell! h dissoc :deadline :on-deadline)\n  nil)\n\n\n(defn queued-bytes\n  \"Bytes handed to `h` and not yet accepted by the kernel: exact, 0 after\n   the terminal.\"\n  [h]\n  (:queued (cell h) 0))\n\n\n(defn pending-tags\n  \"The tags of `h`'s pending segments in FIFO order (:pong, :close, or nil\n   for data), with the head's offset: `[[tag offset len] ...]`.\"\n  [h]\n  (mapv (fn [{:keys [tag off bytes]}] [tag off (alength bytes)]) (:pending (cell h))))\n\n\n(defn close-record\n  [h]\n  (:close (cell h)))\n\n\n(defn read-interest?\n  \"Whether `h` is asking to be read.\"\n  [h]\n  (let [{:keys [reading? pongs remainder]} (cell h)]\n    (boolean (and reading? (< pongs max-pongs) (nil? remainder)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Release and terminals\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- release!\n  \"Close `h`'s descriptor and drop it from the registry with `status`; true\n   when it was open.\"\n  [p h status]\n  (when (open? h)\n    (cell! h assoc :status status :pending [] :queued 0 :remainder nil)\n    (when (registered? p h)\n      (swap! (st p) update :handles dissoc (:fd h)))\n    (tcp/shutdown! (:fd h) :both)\n    (tcp/close! (:fd h))\n    true))\n\n\n(defn discard!\n  \"Release `h` now: descriptor closed, queue dropped, `queued-bytes` 0, no\n   callback. Idempotent.\"\n  [p h]\n  (release! p h :closed)\n  (maybe-release! p)\n  nil)\n\n\n(defn- status-code\n  [peer-status]\n  (case peer-status :absent 1005 :invalid 1002 peer-status))\n\n\n(defn- terminal\n  \"The terminal `{:code :reason}` for close record `c` and `cause`.\"\n  [c cause]\n  (let [{:keys [initiator peer peer-status code reason peer-reason]} c]\n    (case cause\n      :handshake (if (= :local initiator)\n                   {:code code, :reason (or reason \"\")}\n                   {:code (status-code peer-status), :reason (or peer-reason \"\")})\n      :fail {:code code, :reason (or reason \"\")}\n      (:eof :reset :transfer) (if (= :received peer)\n                                {:code (status-code peer-status), :reason (or peer-reason \"\")}\n                                {:code 1006, :reason \"\"})\n      {:code 1006, :reason \"\"})))\n\n\n(defn- terminate!\n  \"End :ws connection `h` once with `cause` (:handshake, :eof, :reset,\n   :transfer, :deadline): descriptor shut, queue released, one owed\n   `:on-closed`.\"\n  [p h cause detail]\n  (let [c (:close (cell h))]\n    (when (release! p h :closed)\n      (let [t (assoc (terminal c cause) :cause cause)\n            t (if detail (assoc t :detail detail) t)]\n        (when-let [f (:on-closed (cell h))]\n          (owe! p #(f t)))))))\n\n\n(defn- fail!\n  \"A transfer failure: a :ws connection terminates per A5, a :raw one owes\n   `:on-error` once with `failure`.\"\n  [p h failure]\n  (if (= :ws (:mode (cell h)))\n    (terminate! p h (if (= :reset (:class failure)) :reset :transfer) failure)\n    (when (release! p h :failed)\n      (when-let [f (:on-error (cell h))]\n        (owe! p #(f failure))))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Writes (A3)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- write-once!\n  \"One send of at most `chunk-bytes` of `bs` from `off`: the bytes the\n   kernel accepted (0 on EAGAIN), or a failure map.\"\n  [p fd bs off]\n  (let [wbuf (:wbuf @(st p))\n        n (min chunk-bytes (- (alength bs) off))]\n    (ffi/write-array wbuf bs off n)\n    (let [r (tcp/send-chunk! fd wbuf n)]\n      (cond\n        (= :would-block r) 0\n        (:error r) r\n        :else (:n r)))))\n\n\n(declare segment-done!)\n\n\n(defn- enqueue!\n  \"Hand whole segment `bs` tagged `tag` to `h`'s FIFO: one write attempt\n   when the FIFO is empty and the connection is established, else append.\n   Answers nil, or `closed-outcome` when the immediate write failed (nothing\n   sent).\"\n  [p h bs tag]\n  (let [{:keys [kind pending]} (cell h)\n        len (alength bs)\n        seg {:bytes bs, :off 0, :tag tag}]\n    (if (or (seq pending) (not= :connection kind))\n      (do (cell! h #(-> % (update :pending conj seg) (update :queued + len)))\n          nil)\n      (let [r (write-once! p (:fd h) bs 0)]\n        (cond\n          (map? r) (do (fail! p h r) closed-outcome)\n          (= r len) (do (segment-done! p h tag) nil)\n          :else (do (cell! h #(-> % (update :pending conj (assoc seg :off r))\n                                  (update :queued + (- len r))))\n                    nil))))))\n\n\n(defn send!\n  \"Hand whole segment `bs` (a byte array the caller no longer mutates) to\n   connection `h`. Answers nil (accepted: on the wire or queued) or\n   `closed-outcome` (failed, closed or closing: nothing sent). A :ws\n   connection refuses data once either side's close exists, so no data\n   frame follows our close frame (RFC 6455 section 5.5.1).\"\n  [p h bs]\n  (let [{:keys [status mode close]} (cell h)]\n    (cond\n      (not= :open status) closed-outcome\n      (and (= :ws mode) (or (:local close) (:peer close))) closed-outcome\n      (zero? (alength bs)) nil\n      :else (let [r (enqueue! p h bs nil)]\n              (maybe-release! p)\n              r))))\n\n\n(defn- drain-once!\n  \"One write of the head segment; answers whether bytes moved.\"\n  [p h]\n  (let [{:keys [bytes off tag]} (first (:pending (cell h)))\n        r (write-once! p (:fd h) bytes off)]\n    (cond\n      (map? r) (do (fail! p h r) true)\n      (zero? r) false\n      :else (let [off' (+ off r)\n                  done? (= off' (alength bytes))]\n              (cell! h #(-> %\n                            (update :queued - r)\n                            (update :pending (fn [q]\n                                               (if done?\n                                                 (subvec q 1)\n                                                 (assoc-in q [0 :off] off'))))))\n              (when done? (segment-done! p h tag))\n              true))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Close (A5) and control output (A4)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- frame\n  \"The wire bytes of a control frame through `h`'s protocol.\"\n  [h opcode payload]\n  (let [{:keys [encode-frame mask-key]} (:protocol (cell h))]\n    (encode-frame (cond-> {:opcode opcode, :payload payload}\n                    mask-key (assoc :mask-key (mask-key))))))\n\n\n(defn close-frame\n  \"The wire bytes of a close frame with `code` and `reason` through `h`'s\n   protocol; a nil `code` is the empty close. 1005 and 1006 are refused:\n   they are reports, never wire codes (RFC 6455 section 7.4.1).\"\n  [h code reason]\n  (when (#{1005 1006} code)\n    (throw (ex-info \"close code 1005/1006 is never serialized\" {:code code})))\n  (frame h :close ((:close-payload (:protocol (cell h))) code reason)))\n\n\n(defn- start-close\n  \"Close record `c` after its first close event: initiator and deadline\n   (`ms` from now, kept if an earlier one was lowered in).\"\n  [c initiator ms]\n  (if (or (:local c) (:peer c))\n    c\n    (let [d (+ (poller/clock-ms) ms)]\n      (assoc c :initiator initiator :deadline (if (:deadline c) (min d (:deadline c)) d)))))\n\n\n(defn close!\n  \"Request the close handshake on :ws connection `h` with `code` and\n   `reason`. The first request wins: a later one, or one after the peer's\n   close was answered, changes nothing. The close frame takes the reserved\n   control slot behind everything already queued.\"\n  [p h code reason]\n  (let [{:keys [status mode close]} (cell h)]\n    (when (and (= :open status) (= :ws mode) (nil? (:local close)))\n      (let [bs (close-frame h code reason)]\n        (cell! h update :close #(-> (start-close % :local (:close-deadline-ms (cell h)))\n                                    (assoc :local :queued :code code :reason reason)))\n        (enqueue! p h bs :close)\n        (maybe-release! p))))\n  nil)\n\n\n(defn- segment-done!\n  [p h tag]\n  (case tag\n    :pong (cell! h update :pongs dec)\n    :close (do (cell! h assoc-in [:close :local] :sent)\n               (cond\n                 (:failed? (:close (cell h))) (terminate! p h :fail nil)\n                 (= :received (:peer (:close (cell h)))) (terminate! p h :handshake nil)))\n    nil)\n  (when (and (open? h) (empty? (:pending (cell h))))\n    (when-let [f (:on-drained (cell h))] (owe! p f))))\n\n\n(defn- fail-connection!\n  \"A parser :fail without :close? fails the connection (RFC 6455 section\n   7.1.7): the close frame takes the reserved slot, later input is\n   discarded unparsed (read only to see EOF or reset) and the unparsed rest\n   is dropped. The peer's reply is never parsed: the terminal is `:fail`\n   with the local code once the frame is written. After a local `close!`\n   the requested frame keeps the slot (no second frame) and its code: still\n   queued, the connection fails once it is written; already sent, it fails\n   now.\"\n  [p h code reason]\n  (let [{:keys [status mode close]} (cell h)]\n    (when (and (= :open status) (= :ws mode) (nil? (:peer close)))\n      (case (:local close)\n        nil (let [bs (close-frame h code reason)]\n              (cell! h #(-> %\n                            (assoc :remainder nil)\n                            (update :close (fn [c]\n                                             (-> (start-close c :local (:close-deadline-ms %))\n                                                 (assoc :local :queued :code code :reason reason\n                                                        :failed? true))))))\n              (enqueue! p h bs :close)\n              (maybe-release! p))\n        :queued (cell! h #(-> %\n                              (assoc :remainder nil)\n                              (assoc-in [:close :failed?] true)))\n        :sent (do (cell! h assoc-in [:close :failed?] true)\n                  (terminate! p h :fail nil)\n                  (maybe-release! p)))))\n  nil)\n\n\n(defn- peer-close!\n  \"The peer's close frame: record it, start the deadline if first, and\n   answer it unless this side's close is already queued or sent.\"\n  [p h status code reason]\n  (cell! h update :close #(-> (start-close % :peer (:close-deadline-ms (cell h)))\n                              (assoc :peer :received :peer-status status :peer-reason reason)))\n  (let [{:keys [local]} (:close (cell h))]\n    (cond\n      (= :sent local) (terminate! p h :handshake nil)\n      (= :queued local) nil\n      :else (let [[rcode rreason] (case status\n                                    :absent [nil nil]\n                                    :invalid [1002 \"dao.stream/protocol-error\"]\n                                    [code nil])]\n              (cell! h update :close assoc :local :queued :code rcode :reason rreason)\n              (enqueue! p h (close-frame h rcode rreason) :close)))))\n\n\n(defn- pong!\n  [p h payload]\n  (cell! h update :pongs inc)\n  (enqueue! p h (frame h :pong payload) :pong))\n\n\n(defn- handle-event!\n  [p h ev]\n  (let [{:keys [local peer]} (:close (cell h))]\n    (case (:type ev)\n      :ping (when (nil? local) (pong! p h (:payload ev)))\n      :pong nil\n      :message (when (and (nil? local) (nil? peer))\n                 (when-let [f (:on-message (cell h))] (f ev)))\n      :close (when (nil? peer)\n               (peer-close! p h (if (nil? (:code ev)) :absent (:code ev)) (:code ev) (:reason ev)))\n      :fail (if (:close? ev)\n              (when (nil? peer) (peer-close! p h :invalid nil nil))\n              (fail-connection! p h (:code ev) (:reason ev)))\n      nil)))\n\n\n(defn- parse-input!\n  \"Parse `chunk` from `offset` one frame at a time until it is consumed,\n   the connection ends, or the pong budget fills; keep the unparsed rest.\"\n  [p h chunk offset]\n  (let [parse (:parse-frame (:protocol (cell h)))\n        n (alength chunk)]\n    (loop [off offset]\n      (cond\n        (not (open? h)) nil\n        (>= off n) (cell! h assoc :remainder nil)\n        (>= (:pongs (cell h)) max-pongs) (cell! h assoc :remainder {:chunk chunk, :offset off})\n        :else (let [[ev parser' off'] (parse (:parser (cell h)) chunk off)]\n                (cell! h assoc :parser parser')\n                (if (nil? ev)\n                  (cell! h assoc :remainder nil)\n                  (do (handle-event! p h ev)\n                      (recur off'))))))))\n\n\n(defn- resume!\n  \"Parse a held remainder once the pong budget has room; answers whether\n   anything was parsed.\"\n  [p h]\n  (when (and (open? h) (< (:pongs (cell h)) max-pongs))\n    (when-let [{:keys [chunk offset]} (:remainder (cell h))]\n      (parse-input! p h chunk offset)\n      true)))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Reads and visits (A1)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- read-once!\n  \"One recv chunk into the reused buffer; answers whether anything\n   happened.\"\n  [p h]\n  (let [rbuf (:rbuf @(st p))\n        r (tcp/recv-chunk! (:fd h) rbuf chunk-bytes)\n        ws? (= :ws (:mode (cell h)))]\n    (cond\n      (= :would-block r) false\n      (= :eof r) (do (if ws?\n                       (terminate! p h :eof nil)\n                       (do (cell! h assoc :reading? false :eof? true)\n                           (when-let [f (:on-eof (cell h))] (owe! p f))))\n                     true)\n      (:error r) (do (fail! p h r) true)\n      :else (let [chunk (ffi/read-array rbuf (:n r))]\n              (if ws?\n                ;; a failed connection reads only to see EOF or reset: the\n                ;; input is discarded, never parsed\n                (when-not (:failed? (:close (cell h))) (parse-input! p h chunk 0))\n                (when-let [f (:on-data (cell h))] (f chunk)))\n              true))))\n\n\n(def ^:private write-ready (bit-or native/pollout native/pollerr native/pollhup native/pollnval))\n\n\n(defn- visit-connection!\n  [p h rev]\n  (let [wrote? (when (and (pos? (bit-and rev write-ready)) (seq (:pending (cell h))))\n                 (drain-once! p h))\n        ;; resume before read: a held remainder is parsed first, and read\n        ;; interest returns only once it is consumed\n        resumed? (resume! p h)\n        read? (when (and (not resumed?) (open? h) (read-interest? h)\n                         (pos? (bit-and rev native/poll-readable)))\n                (read-once! p h))]\n    (boolean (or wrote? resumed? read?))))\n\n\n(defn- visit-connecting!\n  [p h rev]\n  (when (pos? rev)\n    (let [r (tcp/connect-result (:fd h))]\n      (if (map? r)\n        (fail! p h r)\n        (do (cell! h assoc :kind :connection)\n            (when-let [f (:on-connected (cell h))] (owe! p f)))))\n    true))\n\n\n(defn- can-accept?\n  [p lh]\n  (let [{:keys [status accepting? max-pending-upgrades max-connections]} (cell lh)\n        mine (owned p lh)]\n    (and (= :open status) accepting?\n         (< (count (filter #(:upgrading? (cell %)) mine)) max-pending-upgrades)\n         (or (nil? max-connections) (< (count mine) max-connections)))))\n\n\n(defn- visit-listener!\n  [p h]\n  (loop [i 0, moved? false]\n    (if (or (= i accept-batch) (not (can-accept? p h)))\n      moved?\n      (let [r (tcp/accept! (:fd h))]\n        (cond\n          (= :would-block r) moved?\n          (:error r) (do (when-let [f (:on-accept-error (cell h))] (owe! p #(f r))) true)\n          ;; :on-accept runs inside the visit: the callee's registration is\n          ;; what the pending-upgrade cap counts. Without one, nobody owns\n          ;; the descriptor, so it is closed rather than leaked.\n          :else (do (if-let [f (:on-accept (cell h))]\n                      (f r)\n                      (tcp/close! (:fd r)))\n                    (recur (inc i) true)))))))\n\n\n(defn interest\n  \"`[[fd events] ...]` for this instance's next poll, registration order.\"\n  [p]\n  (vec (keep (fn [h]\n               (let [{:keys [kind pending]} (cell h)\n                     ev (case kind\n                          :listener (if (can-accept? p h) native/pollin 0)\n                          :connecting native/pollout\n                          :connection (bit-or (if (read-interest? h) native/pollin 0)\n                                              (if (seq pending) native/pollout 0)))]\n                 (when (pos? ev) [(:fd h) ev])))\n             (sort-by :id (vals (:handles @(st p)))))))\n\n\n(defn- runnable-remainder?\n  [h]\n  (let [{:keys [remainder pongs]} (cell h)]\n    (and remainder (< pongs max-pongs))))\n\n\n(defn next-deadline\n  \"The nearest due time on `jolt.io-poller/clock-ms`: now while a report is\n   owed or a held remainder can be parsed; else the nearest upgrade, close\n   or registered deadline; nil when nothing is due (an empty registry).\"\n  [p]\n  (let [{:keys [handles notify closed-listeners]} @(st p)\n        hs (vals handles)\n        ;; a closed listener is due only once its drain report is owed: while\n        ;; it still owns connections, their own deadlines bound the wait\n        drained (some (fn [[_ lh]] (empty? (owned p lh))) closed-listeners)]\n    (if (or (seq notify) drained (some runnable-remainder? hs))\n      (poller/clock-ms)\n      (let [ds (mapcat (fn [h]\n                         (let [c (cell h)]\n                           (keep identity [(:deadline c) (:upgrade-deadline c)\n                                           (:deadline (:close c))])))\n                       hs)]\n        (when (seq ds) (reduce min ds))))))\n\n\n(defn- deliver-notify!\n  [p]\n  (let [owed (:notify @(st p))]\n    (swap! (st p) assoc :notify [])\n    (doseq [f owed] (f))\n    (boolean (seq owed))))\n\n\n(defn- expire!\n  \"Run every due deadline; answers whether any fired.\"\n  [p]\n  (let [now (poller/clock-ms)\n        due? #(and % (<= % now))\n        fired (atom false)]\n    (doseq [h (sort-by :id (vals (:handles @(st p))))]\n      (let [c (cell h)]\n        (cond\n          (not (registered? p h)) nil\n          (due? (:upgrade-deadline c))\n          (do (reset! fired true)\n              (release! p h :closed)\n              (when-let [f (:on-upgrade-expired c)] (owe! p f)))\n          (due? (:deadline (:close c)))\n          (do (reset! fired true)\n              (cond\n                (= :ws (:mode c)) (terminate! p h :deadline nil)\n                (:upgrading? c) (do (release! p h :closed)\n                                    (when-let [f (:on-upgrade-expired c)] (owe! p f)))\n                ;; a plain :raw stream has no upgrade to expire: its lowered\n                ;; deadline ends it as a failure\n                :else (fail! p h {:error :close-deadline, :class :other})))\n          (due? (:deadline c))\n          (let [f (:on-deadline c)]\n            (reset! fired true)\n            (cell! h dissoc :deadline :on-deadline)\n            (when f (f))))))\n    @fired))\n\n\n(defn- report-drained!\n  [p]\n  (doseq [[id lh] (:closed-listeners @(st p))]\n    (when (empty? (owned p lh))\n      (swap! (st p) update :closed-listeners dissoc id)\n      (when-let [f (:on-drained (cell lh))] (owe! p f)))))\n\n\n(defn- rotation\n  \"The handles named in `ready`, in registration order starting after the\n   id visited last.\"\n  [p ready]\n  (let [{:keys [handles last-id]} @(st p)\n        revents (reduce (fn [m [fd rev]] (update m fd (fnil bit-or 0) rev)) {} ready)\n        hs (sort-by (comp :id first)\n                    (keep (fn [[fd rev]]\n                            (when-let [h (get handles fd)]\n                              (when (pos? rev) [h rev])))\n                          revents))\n        after? #(> (:id (first %)) last-id)]\n    (concat (filter after? hs) (remove after? hs))))\n\n\n(defn pump!\n  \"One bounded turn over `ready` (`[[fd revents] ...]` from the driver's\n   poll; `[]` after a timeout): owed reports, one visit per ready\n   descriptor, held remainders that can resume, due expiries, owed reports.\n   Answers `{:progressed? boolean}`.\"\n  [p ready]\n  (let [s (st p)]\n    (try\n      (let [notified? (deliver-notify! p)\n            turn (vec (rotation p ready))\n            visited (doall\n                      (for [[h rev] turn\n                            ;; a descriptor released earlier in this turn,\n                            ;; or its number reused, is not visited\n                            :when (registered? p h)]\n                        (let [moved? (case (:kind (cell h))\n                                       :listener (visit-listener! p h)\n                                       :connecting (visit-connecting! p h rev)\n                                       :connection (visit-connection! p h rev))]\n                          (swap! s assoc :last-id (:id h))\n                          moved?)))\n            seen (set (map (comp :id first) turn))\n            resumed (doall\n                      (for [h (sort-by :id (vals (:handles @s)))\n                            :when (and (not (seen (:id h))) (runnable-remainder? h))]\n                        (resume! p h)))\n            fired? (expire! p)\n            _ (report-drained! p)\n            late? (deliver-notify! p)]\n        {:progressed? (boolean (or notified? (some true? visited) (some true? resumed)\n                                   fired? late?))})\n      (finally\n        (maybe-release! p)))))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Listener lifecycle (A6 seam)\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn close-listener!\n  \"Close listener `lh`'s descriptor (no more accepts). Connections it owns\n   stay; `drained?` turns true once none is left, and `:on-drained` is owed\n   once then.\"\n  [p lh]\n  (when (release! p lh :closed)\n    (swap! (st p) assoc-in [:closed-listeners (:id lh)] lh))\n  (maybe-release! p)\n  nil)\n\n\n(defn drained?\n  \"True when listener `lh`'s descriptor is closed and no connection it owns\n   is left.\"\n  [p lh]\n  (and (not (open? lh)) (empty? (owned p lh))))\n\n\n(defn lower-deadlines!\n  \"Bring the close deadline of every connection listener `lh` owns to at\n   most monotonic `at-ms`, setting one where none exists. Expiry is the\n   A5 deadline: shut, 1006.\"\n  [p lh at-ms]\n  (doseq [h (owned p lh)]\n    (cell! h update-in [:close :deadline] #(if % (min % at-ms) at-ms)))\n  nil)\n\n\n(defn host-pump\n  \"The instance as the driver's `:pump` value (A2): `:interest`,\n   `:deadline` (clock-ms or nil) and `:pump!`.\"\n  [p]\n  {:interest #(interest p)\n   :deadline #(next-deadline p)\n   :pump! #(pump! p %)})\n\n\n(defn debug-state\n  \"This instance's registry for diagnostics: its descriptors, whether its\n   native buffers are held, and owed reports.\"\n  [p]\n  (let [{:keys [handles rbuf notify]} @(st p)]\n    {:fds (vec (sort (keys handles)))\n     :buffers? (some? rbuf)\n     :notify (count notify)}))\nAGENTS.md\ncollab/jolt/1792490170000-reviewer-jolt-j0a-os-j0b-branch.qwen3.8-max.findings.md\ncollab/jolt/1792430106000-reviewer-jolt-j0b-udp-probe-r2.glm-5.3.prompt.md\ncollab/jolt/1792487422000-reviewer-jolt-j0b-linux.qwen3.8-max.findings.md\ncollab/jolt/1792429520000-reviewer-jolt-j0b-udp-probe.glm-5.3.prompt.md\ncollab/jolt/1792489765000-engineer-jolt-j0b-commit-prep.claude-opus-5-5.prompt.md\ncollab/jolt/1792487422000-reviewer-jolt-j0b-linux.qwen3.8-max.prompt.md\ncollab/jolt/1792429249000-engineer-jolt-j0b-udp-probe.claude-opus-5-5.prompt.md\ncollab/jolt/1792429967000-engineer-jolt-j0b-udp-probe-r2.claude-opus-5-5.prompt.md\ncollab/jolt/1792430106000-reviewer-jolt-j0b-udp-probe-r2.glm-5.3.findings.md\ncollab/jolt/1792485587000-engineer-jolt-j0b-linux-port.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md\ncollab/jolt/1792429249000-engineer-jolt-j0b-udp-probe.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md\ncollab/jolt/1792490170000-reviewer-jolt-j0a-os-j0b-branch.qwen3.8-max.prompt.md\ncollab/jolt/1794773141000-engineer-j7-udp-dht.prompt.md\ncollab/jolt/1792429520000-reviewer-jolt-j0b-udp-probe.glm-5.3.findings.md\ncollab/jolt/1792489765000-engineer-jolt-j0b-commit-prep.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md\ncollab/jolt/1792429967000-engineer-jolt-j0b-udp-probe-r2.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md\ncollab/jolt/1794773141000-architect-j7-udp-dht.prompt.md\ncollab/jolt/1792485587000-engineer-jolt-j0b-linux-port.claude-opus-5-5.prompt.md\nsrc/dev/jolt_udp_probe.clj\nsrc/dev/jolt_udp_probe.sh\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
