{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_20","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat src/jolt/README.md && cat src/jolt/dao/host/sign.jolt && cat src/jolt/yin/vm/linker/random.jolt && cat src/jolt/dao/stream/ws/crypto.jolt && cat src/jolt/yin/repl/host.jolt'","aggregated_output":"# src/jolt\n\nThe jolt host source root (docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §4.1-4.2).\nThe `:jolt` alias in `deps.edn` puts it before `src/cljc`, so a namespace here\noverrides the portable one on jolt only.\n\nOnly complete, narrow host implementations live here, as `.jolt` files with the\nsame public surface and contract tests as the portable namespace they replace.\nSmall representation differences go in `#?(:jolt ...)` branches of the shared\n`.cljc` source instead (`:cljd` first, then `:jolt`, then `:clj`/`:default`),\nexcept where the owner has ruled otherwise: the `dao.host.*` profile takes no\n`:jolt` arm in its shared files, and jolt supplies whole-file overrides here\n(J1). Never a copy of `yin.repl`, `yin.vm.engine` or `dao.jing.cbor`.\n\n`dao.host.*` is the portable profile only: a `src/cljc/dao/host/*.cljc`\nnamespace plus its per-host overrides, each entry stated by law, kind and\noracle (docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md §4.2, table A). `dao.posix.*`\nis jolt-only descriptor plumbing over a project-owned native wrapper: it has\nno `.cljc` counterpart and no profile entry, and it is reached only from\n`:jolt` arms, never from a `:default` arm. A jolt-only native facade never\ntakes a `dao.host` name; J4b's lock and filesystem entries join\n`dao.posix.file` or a sibling under `dao.posix`.\n\nThe `.jolt` extension keeps these files out of JVM, ClojureScript and Dart\ntooling (clj-kondo does not lint them as `.cljc`); `.cljstyle` lists `jolt`\namong its extensions so the formatter still checks them.\n\nContents (J0c–J4d):\n- `dao/host/{integer,bytes,text,float,number}.jolt`: the primitive profile\n  (docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md §4.2, table A) over Chez's exact\n  integers, flonums, strings and jolt byte arrays.\n  `bb test:jolt-text-perf` guards that `text`'s code-unit walks stay linear.\n- `clojure/data/json.jolt`: the subset of `clojure.data.json` the test\n  fixtures call, because the library itself does not load on jolt.\n- `yang/antlr/cst.jolt` (J0c round 2): the native-library facade over the\n  ANTLR4 C++ Python3 parser (§7.3). Its contract is the JVM oracle corpus in\n  `src/dev/jolt_j0c_parity.clj`.\n  J5 (known-gaps row 59): the library loads on first use and a refusal is\n  retried by the next selection (`available?`); the wrapper refuses before\n  parsing an input over 4 MiB (status -6) or nesting deeper than 256\n  (status -7, clamped down on a smaller stack), by a flat prescan of the\n  grammar's recursive carriers, answered as the qualified `:parser-budget`\n  diagnostic; convenience calls dispose their workers and the parser stage\n  owns its own (`close-stage`, `reset-stage`). Gates: `bb probe:jolt-j5`.\n- `dao/host/{hash,sign}.jolt` (J1b): BLAKE3, SHA-256 and Ed25519 over\n  `native/jolt-crypto` (§6.2-6.3), built by `bb build:jolt-native` from the\n  pinned BLAKE3 C reference and the declared system libcrypto. Contract: the\n  hash and sign cases of `bb compare:jolt-host`; gates: `bb\n  probe:jolt-crypto`. The wrapper exports nine narrow entries (the J1b eight\n  plus `jolt_crypto_random`, J2); its layout version is 3. Where each\n  libcrypto check runs:\n  - `bb bootstrap:jolt`: the Debian `:os-packages :debian :required` set\n    only (`libssl-dev` among them), with dpkg-query;\n  - `bb build:jolt-native`: the declared path and headers exist; the Debian\n    runtime package (`libssl3t64` or `libssl3`) is installed; the library's\n    canonical path and file SHA-256 equal `:native :libcrypto :qualified`;\n  - loading `dao.host.hash`: the wrapper's layout, then the library its EVP\n    entries resolved to must equal `:qualified` by canonical path, exact\n    version and file SHA-256, the declared path must resolve to it, and its\n    version must be at least `:min-version`.\n  A changed library is requalified with `bb build:jolt-native --requalify`\n  and `bb qualify:jolt-libcrypto` (the procedure is in `jolt-toolchain.edn`).\n\n- `yin/vm/linker/random.jolt` (J2): `rand-bytes` over the wrapper's\n  `jolt_crypto_random` (libcrypto RAND_bytes), used by\n  `yin.vm.linker.sign/generate` on jolt. Requires `dao.host.hash` first so\n  the provenance gate passes before any draw; entropy failure throws.\n- `dao/stream/cbor/boring.jolt` (J2): canonical CBOR writer and strict\n  decoder over jolt primitives, replacing the JVM Boring adapter. The\n  decoder re-encodes to refuse non-canonical bytes; `bb test:jolt-cbor-parity`\n  compares the JVM and jolt bytes against the same cases.\n- `dao/posix/file.jolt` (J4a): POSIX descriptor I/O for `dao.jing.file`'s\n  eight file seams (its `:jolt` arms), over `native/jolt-file`, which `bb\n  build:jolt-native` builds with the pinned CMake. The wrapper is layout 1\n  and exports exactly 13 `jolt_file_*` entries (the allowlist in\n  `native/jolt-file/exports.{darwin,linux}`; the build fails on any other\n  export set). It links system libc only, and its digest is in\n  `build/native/<platform>/build-record.edn :jolt-file`. The s32BE frame\n  prefix is packed in C. The read and write loops are the facade's, behind\n  single-transfer vars that the gates redefine; there is no production fault\n  mode. Loading the namespace refuses a missing or wrong-layout wrapper.\n  Contract: `dao.jing.file-test` on the jolt lane. Gates: `bb\n  probe:jolt-file` (JVM-identical frame bytes, F2 prefixes, strict reads,\n  injected transfers, corrupt-open byte identity, idempotent close, no fd or\n  native-memory leak, GC during a `:blocking` call, missing/wrong wrapper,\n  export allowlist).\n  J4b (layout 2, 22 exports): `dao.space.store.fs`'s `:jolt` arms reach\n  the same namespace for the directory lock (a whole-file `fcntl(F_SETLK)`\n  write lock, close-on-exec, released by its one close; the `held` registry\n  stays in `fs.cljc`), `realpath`, the HEAD replacement (O_EXCL temp at\n  0666 & ~umask, write-all, fsync, checked close, rename, directory\n  open+fsync+close), and bounded reads (classify by lstat/stat, an\n  O_NONBLOCK open rechecked by fstat, strict UTF-8 through `text.jolt`).\n  `directory-sync-supported?` is true on jolt. `bb probe:jolt-file` also\n  gates jolt<->jolt, jolt<->JVM and jolt<->Dart lock contention, SIGKILL\n  recovery, O_CLOEXEC in an exec'd child, the HEAD cut matrix and the\n  bounded-read refusals. Mixed Node/OS-lock ownership is the known-gaps P2\n  row: not claimed.\n  J4c: the content-log handle is fail-closed after a torn append or a\n  failed fsync (review F3; a jolt-only divergence, spec §5 Files row): every\n  later append through that descriptor throws naming the path and the\n  operation, get keeps serving, close closes once, and a reopen's validated\n  recovery decides what stands. A failed fsync is never retried. The file\n  journal and the UCF suites run over it unchanged; the kill-mid-append and\n  the composition's no-yin.repl tests gain a jolt-child leg.\n  J4d (layout 3, 24 exports): `jolt_file_open_private` creates a key file\n  O_EXCL at mode 0600 (`write-new-private!`, answering `:exists` and writing\n  nothing for an existing file), used by `yin.repl.main`'s keygen arm;\n  `yin.repl.state`'s state-file read and the jolt `-main`'s saved flags run\n  over the same namespace. `bb probe:jolt-file` adds the key file's mode and\n  overwrite refusal, saved state through the real jolt `-main`, and\n  cross-host reopen of a `file:` store (JVM, Node and Dart write, jolt\n  replays and appends, the original host reopens).\n\nThe toolchain pins the owner's fork, `https://github.com/sonwh98/jolt`, at\n`697b91eefabf3e964acea787cbc9cb1b6b85a28c` (`jolt-toolchain.edn`). Fork PR\n`sonwh98/jolt#2` carries the Sorted user-type fix (source `7b7100c1`, regenerated\nseeds `697b91ee`), so bootstrap applies no patches and does no re-mint. The former\n`0001-sorted-user-types` patch against `96d7b866` is retired; the verified patch\nmechanism remains available for future patches. `bb test:jolt-bootstrap-neg`\nuses its own temporary synthetic patch to exercise the refusal gates.\n\nJ2 round-3 safety: `dao/stream/cbor.jolt` overrides the public gate; its admission\nexceptions are recorded in [`dao/stream/cbor.reviewed.edn`](dao/stream/cbor.reviewed.edn)\nunder `:parity-exceptions`. Canonical keys/elements retain their\nencoded bytes and must increase strictly, including byte-array duplicates.\n`src/dev/jolt_cbor_safety.clj` exercises performance and main/worker boundaries.\nThe crypto provenance gate now checks all 19 libcrypto symbol origins, including\n`RAND_bytes`; the exported wrapper surface remains nine entries, layout 3.\nSeeds use native nonallocating cleanse before free, including failed draws.\n\n`bb test:jolt-cbor-override` is a babashka-only review gate, also run before\nJ2 list/core/probe/jvm work: it checks the exact shared-source SHA-256 recorded\nin `dao/stream/cbor.reviewed.edn`, public vars and literal argument vectors,\nand every public def initializer. Extraction reads top-level defn/def forms for each\nhost reader branch without evaluating them; it cannot infer macro-generated\nvars or alias arities. On drift, diff the whole-file override, run\n`bb test:jolt-cbor-parity` (emit/check) and `dao.stream.cbor-test` on jolt,\nreview admission/refusal parity including the recorded `:parity-exceptions`, then\nacknowledge that review with `bb test:jolt-cbor-override --update \"<who, date>\"`. Document any\nAPI exception with exact shared/jolt signatures and a reason in the explicit\n`:api-allowlist` (currently empty).\n\nJ3 local shell (macOS verification; Linux unrun):\n\n```sh\nbb bootstrap:jolt\nbb build:jolt-native\n. build/tools/jolt-env.sh\n\"$JOLT_HOME/bin/jolt\" -Srepro -M:jolt:jolt-yin-repl\nbb demo:jolt\nbb test:jolt-shell\nbb test:jolt-core --only '^yin\\.repl'\n```\n\n`dao/stream/waitset/driver.jolt` owns a nonblocking self-pipe. One plain\nthread owns the shell; native poll waits for stdin or a wake at the cadence\ndeadline. Chez's buffered stdin is checked before waiting on fd 0. A partial\nline without newline blocks that owner until newline. Ctrl-C exits without a\ngraceful drain, as on the JVM. There is no line editor.\n\nThe standard catalog installs Clojure, PHP and (since J5) Python over the\nnative C++ parser; with no usable parser library Python is left out and\n`(lang :yang/python)` answers unavailable-parser, never another parser;\nfile: stores open since J4 (J4d); dht: stores refuse at composition until J7.\nSaved CLI state is enabled by the Jolt entry point since J4d. Networking has no host adapter\nuntil J6/J7. Source startup is measured by the transcript runner; no speed\ngate is applied. The same corpus has a JVM process test. Capability-only\ncases identify their host in the corpus, rather than binding a JVM socket or\nopening a durable JVM store just to prove a Jolt refusal.\n\nPer-deftest decisions: `test/resources/yin/repl/jolt-applicability.edn`.\nThe shared reader tests remain active: extra source metadata and accepted\nunknown tags/reader conditionals are failures, not accepted divergences.\nOnly the two explicitly tagged transcript reader rows (small `N` literals\nand lone surrogate escapes) have host-specific expected results.\n(ns dao.host.sign\n  \"The jolt override of src/cljc/dao/host/sign.cljc (the :jolt alias puts\n   src/jolt first, docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §4.2, §6.3):\n   the same public entries, arities, docstrings and refusal data\n   (docs/design/dao.stream.host-boundary.md §4.2, table A).\n\n   P entries (§2.4):\n   - `keypair-from-seed`: takes 32-byte seed (host bytes), returns\n     `{:seed <32 bytes>, :public <32 bytes>}` as fresh host bytes.\n   - `sign`: takes 32-byte seed and arbitrary message (host bytes),\n     returns 64-byte Ed25519 signature as fresh host bytes.\n   - `verify`: takes 32-byte public key, arbitrary message, and 64-byte signature\n     (all host bytes), returns boolean (true if valid, false otherwise).\n\n   Deterministic seed replay through the declared system libcrypto's raw\n   Ed25519 keys (EVP_PKEY_new_raw_private_key, one-shot EVP_DigestSign and\n   EVP_DigestVerify), behind native/jolt-crypto. The library and its\n   provenance gate are dao.host.hash's: requiring it first means no key is\n   used before that check passes.\n\n   Each native acquisition owns its own release, established as soon as it\n   is acquired, so a later allocation or copy that fails still releases\n   every earlier buffer, and each release runs whether or not another\n   failed. Native memory that held seed bytes is zeroed by the wrapper's\n   jolt_crypto_cleanse (OPENSSL_cleanse, which allocates nothing) before it\n   is freed, on every path, including a seed copy that failed part way.\n   Fresh entropy key generation (`generate`) is deferred (§9.1) and remains\n   in `yin.vm.linker.sign`.\"\n  (:require\n    [clojure.string :as str]\n    [dao.host.bytes :as bytes]\n    ;; loaded for its provenance gate, which must pass before any call here\n    [dao.host.hash]\n    [jolt.ffi :as ffi]))\n\n\n(def ^:private library\n  ;; the same wrapper dao.host.hash loaded and qualified (jolt's os.name and\n  ;; os.arch shim can answer nil, which reads as Linux and x86_64)\n  (let [os (str/lower-case (or (System/getProperty \"os.name\") \"\"))\n        arch (System/getProperty \"os.arch\")\n        darwin? (str/includes? os \"mac\")]\n    (str (System/getProperty \"user.dir\") \"/build/native/\"\n         (if darwin? \"darwin\" \"linux\") \"-\"\n         (if (#{\"aarch64\" \"arm64\"} arch) \"arm64\" \"x86_64\")\n         \"/jolt-crypto/lib/libjolt_crypto\" (if darwin? \".dylib\" \".so\"))))\n\n\n(ffi/load-library library)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-public \"jolt_crypto_ed25519_public\"\n  [:pointer :pointer] :int :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-sign \"jolt_crypto_ed25519_sign\"\n  [:pointer :pointer :size_t :pointer] :int :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-verify \"jolt_crypto_ed25519_verify\"\n  [:pointer :pointer :size_t :pointer] :int :blocking)\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-cleanse \"jolt_crypto_cleanse\" [:pointer :size_t] :void)\n\n\n(defn- check-bytes!\n  [entry name-kw bs expected-len]\n  (when-not (bytes/bytes? bs)\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"dao.host refused: \" entry \" unsupported-value (expected \" (name name-kw) \" host bytes)\")\n                    {:dao.host/refusal :unsupported-value\n                     :dao.host/entry entry\n                     :target name-kw})))\n  (when (and expected-len (not= (bytes/length bs) expected-len))\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"dao.host refused: \" entry \" out-of-range (expected \" expected-len \" bytes, got \" (bytes/length bs) \")\")\n                    {:dao.host/refusal :out-of-range\n                     :dao.host/entry entry\n                     :expected expected-len\n                     :actual (bytes/length bs)}))))\n\n\n(defn- acquire\n  \"Caller-owned native memory of `n` bytes (at least one); throws when the\n   allocator answers NULL, so a caller never holds an unowned pointer.\"\n  [n]\n  (let [p (ffi/alloc (max 1 n))]\n    (when (ffi/null? p)\n      (throw (ex-info \"jolt-crypto native allocation failed\" {:bytes n})))\n    p))\n\n\n(defn- with-native\n  \"(f p) over `n` bytes of native memory, freed however `f` exits.\"\n  [n f]\n  (let [p (acquire n)]\n    (try (f p) (finally (ffi/free p)))))\n\n\n(defn- with-copy\n  \"(f p) over a native copy of `bs`, freed however the copy or `f` exits.\"\n  [bs f]\n  (let [n (alength bs)]\n    (with-native n\n      (fn [p]\n        (when (pos? n) (ffi/write-array p bs))\n        (f p)))))\n\n\n(defn- with-secret\n  \"(f p) over a native copy of secret `bs`; the memory is cleansed and then\n   freed however the copy or `f` exits, and freed even if cleansing throws.\"\n  [bs f]\n  (let [n (alength bs)\n        p (acquire n)]\n    (try\n      (ffi/write-array p bs)\n      (f p)\n      (finally\n        (try (c-cleanse p n)\n             (finally (ffi/free p)))))))\n\n\n(defn- fail\n  [entry status]\n  (throw (ex-info (str \"jolt-crypto \" entry \" failed\") {:entry entry, :status status})))\n\n\n(defn keypair-from-seed\n  \"P. Deterministic Ed25519 keypair from 32-byte seed `seed-bytes`.\n   Returns `{:seed <32-byte host bytes>, :public <32-byte host bytes>}`.\"\n  [seed-bytes]\n  (check-bytes! 'keypair-from-seed :seed seed-bytes 32)\n  (let [seed-copy (bytes/slice seed-bytes 0 32)]\n    (with-secret seed-copy\n      (fn [seed]\n        (with-native 32\n          (fn [out]\n            (let [status (c-public seed out)]\n              (when-not (zero? status) (fail 'keypair-from-seed status))\n              {:seed seed-copy\n               :public (ffi/read-array out 32)})))))))\n\n\n(defn sign\n  \"P. Sign message `msg-bytes` with 32-byte `seed-bytes`.\n   Returns 64-byte Ed25519 signature as fresh host bytes.\"\n  [seed-bytes msg-bytes]\n  (check-bytes! 'sign :seed seed-bytes 32)\n  (check-bytes! 'sign :message msg-bytes nil)\n  (with-secret seed-bytes\n    (fn [seed]\n      (with-copy msg-bytes\n        (fn [msg]\n          (with-native 64\n            (fn [out]\n              (let [status (c-sign seed msg (alength msg-bytes) out)]\n                (when-not (zero? status) (fail 'sign status))\n                (ffi/read-array out 64)))))))))\n\n\n(defn verify\n  \"P. Verify 64-byte `sig-bytes` against `msg-bytes` and 32-byte `public-key-bytes`.\n   Returns boolean.\"\n  [public-key-bytes msg-bytes sig-bytes]\n  (check-bytes! 'verify :public-key public-key-bytes 32)\n  (check-bytes! 'verify :message msg-bytes nil)\n  (check-bytes! 'verify :signature sig-bytes 64)\n  (with-copy public-key-bytes\n    (fn [pub]\n      (with-copy msg-bytes\n        (fn [msg]\n          (with-copy sig-bytes\n            (fn [sig]\n              ;; 1 valid; 0 invalid; a negative wrapper failure after\n              ;; validation is false, as the .cljc arms' catch makes it\n              (= 1 (c-verify pub msg (alength msg-bytes) sig)))))))))\n(ns yin.vm.linker.random\n  \"The jolt-only fresh-entropy adapter behind `yin.vm.linker.sign/generate`'s\n   `:jolt` arm (owner ruling of 2026-10-22, collab/1792655000000).\n\n   Fresh key generation stays in `yin.vm.linker.sign` (the deferred entropy\n   design, yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md 9.1); this namespace is the narrow\n   bridge that makes its jolt arm draw seed bytes from the SAME qualified\n   system libcrypto every dao.host.hash / dao.host.sign call already resolved\n   to, through the native/jolt-crypto wrapper's `jolt_crypto_random`\n   (libcrypto RAND_bytes). jolt's own java.security.SecureRandom shim\n   (host/chez/java/host-static-classes.ss, /dev/urandom backed) exists and\n   works at this pin, but it sits outside the provenance gate, so this\n   namespace requires `dao.host.hash` first — no seed byte is drawn before\n   that gate has passed, exactly the dao.host.sign discipline — and a failed\n   draw is an error (ex-info), never a silent substitute.\"\n  (:require\n    [clojure.string :as str]\n    [dao.host.bytes :as bytes]\n    ;; loaded for its provenance gate, which must pass before any draw\n    [dao.host.hash]\n    [jolt.ffi :as ffi]))\n\n\n(def ^:private library\n  ;; the same wrapper dao.host.hash loaded and qualified (jolt's os.name and\n  ;; os.arch shim can answer nil, which reads as Linux and x86_64)\n  (let [os (str/lower-case (or (System/getProperty \"os.name\") \"\"))\n        arch (System/getProperty \"os.arch\")\n        darwin? (str/includes? os \"mac\")]\n    (str (System/getProperty \"user.dir\") \"/build/native/\"\n         (if darwin? \"darwin\" \"linux\") \"-\"\n         (if (#{\"aarch64\" \"arm64\"} arch) \"arm64\" \"x86_64\")\n         \"/jolt-crypto/lib/libjolt_crypto\" (if darwin? \".dylib\" \".so\"))))\n\n\n(ffi/load-library library)\n\n\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-random \"jolt_crypto_random\"\n            [:pointer :size_t] :int :blocking)\n\n\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-cleanse \"jolt_crypto_cleanse\" [:pointer :size_t] :void)\n\n\n(defn rand-bytes\n  \"A fresh jolt byte array of `n` random bytes (n >= 0) from the qualified\n   libcrypto's CSPRNG. Randomness failure is an error: a nonzero wrapper\n   status throws.\"\n  [n]\n  (when (neg? n)\n    (throw (ex-info \"yin.vm.linker.random/rand-bytes negative count\" {:n n})))\n  (let [size (max 1 n)\n        p (ffi/alloc size)]\n    (try\n      (when (ffi/null? p)\n        (throw (ex-info \"jolt-crypto native allocation failed\" {:bytes n})))\n      (let [status (c-random p n)]\n        (when-not (zero? status)\n          (throw (ex-info \"jolt-crypto random failed\" {:entry 'rand-bytes\n                                                       :status status})))\n        (if (pos? n) (ffi/read-array p n) (bytes/alloc 0)))\n      (finally\n        (try (c-cleanse p size)\n             (finally (ffi/free p)))))))\n(ns dao.stream.ws.crypto\n  \"The jolt-only digest and entropy the RFC 6455 core needs, injected into\n   it by the jolt adapter (docs/design/yin.repl-jolt-implementation.md §9;\n   J6 plan v2 A5, A7). The portable core takes both as arguments and never\n   reaches a host; this namespace is where jolt's come from.\n\n   `sha1` is the Sec-WebSocket-Accept digest of the opening handshake\n   (RFC 6455 §4.2.2) and nothing else: SHA-1 is not a content, integrity or\n   identity hash anywhere in the system (those are BLAKE3, `dao.host.hash`).\n   `mask-key` draws the four masking-key bytes of a client frame (§5.3).\n\n   Both call the native/jolt-crypto wrapper (layout 4: `jolt_crypto_sha1`,\n   EVP_sha1 from the system libcrypto, and `jolt_crypto_random`, its\n   RAND_bytes), the same wrapper and the same qualified library every\n   `dao.host.hash` and `dao.host.sign` call resolved to. This namespace\n   requires `dao.host.hash` first, so its load-time gate (layout, then\n   provenance over all twenty libcrypto entries) has passed before any\n   symbol here is bound or any byte digested or drawn; a stale, missing or\n   unqualified wrapper, or one lacking either entry, fails the load. A failed digest or draw throws\n   (ex-info), never a silent substitute.\"\n  (:require\n    [clojure.string :as str]\n    [dao.host.bytes :as bytes]\n    ;; loaded for its gate, which must pass before anything below binds\n    [dao.host.hash]\n    [jolt.ffi :as ffi]))\n\n\n(def ^:private library\n  ;; the wrapper dao.host.hash loaded and qualified (jolt's os.name and\n  ;; os.arch shim can answer nil, which reads as Linux and x86_64)\n  (let [os (str/lower-case (or (System/getProperty \"os.name\") \"\"))\n        arch (System/getProperty \"os.arch\")\n        darwin? (str/includes? os \"mac\")]\n    (str (System/getProperty \"user.dir\") \"/build/native/\"\n         (if darwin? \"darwin\" \"linux\") \"-\"\n         (if (#{\"aarch64\" \"arm64\"} arch) \"arm64\" \"x86_64\")\n         \"/jolt-crypto/lib/libjolt_crypto\" (if darwin? \".dylib\" \".so\"))))\n\n\n(ffi/load-library library)\n\n\n;; defcfn binds lazily (a missing entry surfaces only at the first call), so\n;; the load refuses here, before guest work, when an entry is absent\n(doseq [entry [\"jolt_crypto_sha1\" \"jolt_crypto_random\"]]\n  (when-not (ffi/find-symbol entry)\n    (throw (ex-info (str \"jolt-crypto refused: the library lacks \" entry)\n                    {:library library, :entry entry}))))\n\n\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-sha1 \"jolt_crypto_sha1\"\n            [:pointer :size_t :pointer] :int :blocking)\n\n\n(ffi/defcfn ^:private c-random \"jolt_crypto_random\"\n            [:pointer :size_t] :int :blocking)\n\n\n(defn- with-native\n  \"(f p) over caller-owned native memory `p` of `n` bytes (one for an empty\n   input), freed however `f` exits.\"\n  [n f]\n  (let [p (ffi/alloc (max 1 n))]\n    (try\n      (when (ffi/null? p)\n        (throw (ex-info \"jolt-crypto native allocation failed\" {:bytes n})))\n      (f p)\n      (finally (ffi/free p)))))\n\n\n(defn sha1\n  \"The 20-byte SHA-1 digest of host bytes `bs`, as fresh host bytes; `bs`\n   is left unmodified. For Sec-WebSocket-Accept only.\"\n  [bs]\n  (when-not (bytes/bytes? bs)\n    (throw (ex-info \"dao.stream.ws.crypto/sha1 expects host bytes\" {:value bs})))\n  (let [n (alength bs)]\n    (with-native n\n      (fn [in]\n        (when (pos? n) (ffi/write-array in bs))\n        (with-native 20\n          (fn [out]\n            (let [status (c-sha1 in n out)]\n              (when-not (zero? status)\n                (throw (ex-info \"jolt-crypto sha1 failed\" {:entry 'sha1, :status status})))\n              (ffi/read-array out 20))))))))\n\n\n(defn mask-key\n  \"Four fresh bytes from the qualified libcrypto's CSPRNG, as host bytes:\n   the masking key of one client frame (RFC 6455 §5.3). A failed draw\n   throws.\"\n  []\n  (with-native 4\n    (fn [p]\n      (let [status (c-random p 4)]\n        (when-not (zero? status)\n          (throw (ex-info \"jolt-crypto random failed\" {:entry 'mask-key, :status status})))\n        (ffi/read-array p 4)))))\n(ns yin.repl.host\n  \"The jolt composition of the Yin REPL host WebSocket seam (J6e).\n\n   This jolt namespace shadows the `.cljc` one, so it carries only\n   `websocket`; the portable contract is `yin.repl.host.common`. It\n   composes the native RFC 6455 edge (`dao.stream.ws.jolt`) for the REPL:\n   upgrades reach `dao.stream.ws/accept-connection!` through the endpoint's\n   `accept!`, and each accepted connection reports through the\n   `dao.stream.ws/adapter` of its handle.\n\n   Unlike the JVM, Node and Dart packages, jolt's sockets move only when\n   their owner pumps them, so the adapter also carries `:pump` (the\n   driver's `{:interest :deadline :pump!}`, A2) and `:close-host!` (forced\n   cleanup once the owner loop has ended, A6). One instance per call: two\n   adapters never share a descriptor.\"\n  (:require [dao.stream.ws :as ws]\n            [dao.stream.ws.jolt :as wsj]))\n\n\n(defn websocket\n  \"Return a fresh jolt host adapter: `{:connect! :bind! :unbind! :pump\n   :close-host! :debug-state}` over one new instance. `opts` (tests only):\n   `:upgrade-timeout-ms`, `:close-deadline-ms`.\"\n  ([] (websocket nil))\n  ([opts]\n   (let [inst (wsj/create opts)]\n     {:connect! (fn [descriptor adapter] (wsj/connect! inst descriptor adapter))\n      :bind! (fn [options]\n               (wsj/bind! inst (assoc options :ws.jolt/adapter-of\n                                      #(some-> (:ws/handle %) ws/adapter))))\n      :unbind! (fn [listener deposit!] (wsj/unbind! inst listener deposit!))\n      :pump (wsj/host-pump inst)\n      :close-host! #(wsj/close-host! inst)\n      :debug-state #(wsj/debug-state inst)})))\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
{"type":"item.started","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '55,160p' src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc && sed -n '250,310p' src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc && sed -n '424,504p' src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc && sed -n '1418,1441p' src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc && cat src/cljc/yin/shell/store.cljc && sed -n '1,100p' src/cljc/yin/shell/main.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"","exit_code":null,"status":"in_progress"}}
{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_21","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc \"sed -n '55,160p' src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc && sed -n '250,310p' src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc && sed -n '424,504p' src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc && sed -n '1418,1441p' src/cljc/dao/space/dht.cljc && cat src/cljc/yin/shell/store.cljc && sed -n '1,100p' src/cljc/yin/shell/main.cljc\"","aggregated_output":"                       [dao.stream.datagram.dart :as datagram.host]]\n                :cljs [[dao.stream.datagram.node :as datagram.host]]\n                :clj [[dao.stream.datagram.jvm :as datagram.host]])\n            [clojure.string :as str]\n            [dao.jing :as jing]\n            [dao.jing.content.step :as content.step]\n            [dao.jing.dht :as dht]\n            [dao.space.index :as index]\n            [dao.space.query :as query]\n            [dao.space.store :as durable]\n            [dao.stream :as stream]\n            [dao.stream.datagram :as datagram]\n            [dao.stream.ringbuffer :as ringbuffer]))\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; Options and defaults\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(def default-max-inbound-bytes\n  \"The default inbound storage bound (`:dao.jing.dht/max-inbound-bytes`):\n   64 MiB of peers' `:store` payloads a publishing node accepts before it\n   refuses them explicitly.\"\n  (* 64 1024 1024))\n\n\n(def max-pending-writes\n  \"Replicate requests the node holds outstanding at most: the DHT's own\n   pending-write bound, so the node never draws `/busy`.\"\n  (::dht/max-pending-writes dht/defaults))\n\n\n(def defaults\n  \"`join`'s option defaults: solo, publishing off, loopback on an\n   ephemeral port, the default inbound bound, and the backlog and repair\n   bounds of yin.vm.linker.dht.md section 11.\"\n  {:peers []\n   :publish? false\n   :bind-host \"127.0.0.1\"\n   :bind-port 0\n   :max-inbound-bytes default-max-inbound-bytes\n   :max-backlog 4096\n   :repair-batch 64\n   :max-open 16\n   :repair-slots 16\n   :repair-ticks 30000\n   :repair-max-ticks 600000\n   :max-repairing 16})\n\n\n(def secret-length\n  \"Bytes of the root secret minted per join: section 8's floor.\"\n  32)\n\n\n(def ring-capacity\n  \"Capacity of each ring the node is composed over: requests, answers,\n   facts, ticks, traffic, and the publication ledger.\"\n  4096)\n\n\n(def step-budget\n  \"The DHT's per-stage budget per step.\"\n  64)\n\n\n(def fetch-budget\n  \"Answers the load client reads per step.\"\n  16)\n\n\n(def retryable\n  \"Failure reasons repair retries (yin.vm.linker.dht.md 5.5.4).  Every\n   other reason is terminal.\"\n  #{::dht/too-few-peers ::dht/busy ::backlog-full ::publications-full})\n\n\n(defn default-bind\n  \"This build's datagram host seam (dao.stream.datagram.md section 3): the\n   JVM's, Node's `dgram` (required only when a socket binds, so a browser\n   build compiles), or Dart's.\"\n  []\n  #?(:cljd datagram.host/bind!\n     :cljs datagram.host/bind!\n     :clj datagram.host/bind!))\n\n\n(defn secure-random-bytes\n  \"`n` bytes from the host's cryptographically secure generator.\"\n  [n]\n  #?(:cljd (let [r (math/Random.secure)]\n             (typed/Uint8List.fromList\n               (vec (repeatedly n #(.nextInt r 256)))))\n     :clj (let [bs (byte-array n)]\n            (.nextBytes (java.security.SecureRandom.) bs)\n            bs)\n     :cljs (.getRandomValues js/crypto (js/Uint8Array. n))))\n\n\n(defn- refused\n  \"A refused call: the error, returned for the caller to throw.  Its data\n   names the closed `code` under `:dao.space.dht/refused`.\"\n  [code message data]\n  (ex-info (str \"dao.space.dht: \" message) (assoc data ::refused code)))\n\n\n(defn- node-over\n  \"The node composed from checked `opts` over the opened `local` store.\"\n  [opts local]\n  (let [{:keys [peers publish? bind-host bind-port max-inbound-bytes bind!]}\n        opts\n        [requests answers facts ticks traffic ledger] (repeatedly 6 ring)\n        socket? (boolean (seq peers))\n        ;; The effective transport configuration, resolved once here and\n        ;; used consistently by every consumer of it: the DHT state, the\n        ;; store front's own oversize refusal, and the payload capability\n        ;; a storage composition declares (`payload-budget`).  Three\n        ;; consumers reading three defaults is how a bound and a budget\n        ;; come to disagree.\n        message-bound (or (::dht/max-message-bytes opts)\n                          (::dht/max-message-bytes dht/defaults))\n        composition (cond-> {:local local\n                             :requests requests\n                             :answers answers\n                             :facts facts\n                             :ticks ticks\n                             ::dht/id (dht/bytes->hex\n                                        (secure-random-bytes dht/id-length))\n                             ::dht/publish? publish?\n                             ::dht/bootstrap (vec peers)\n                             ::dht/bind-host bind-host}\n                      socket? (assoc :traffic traffic\n                                     ::dht/secret (secure-random-bytes\n                                                    secret-length)\n                                     ::dht/max-inbound-bytes max-inbound-bytes))\n        seam (when socket?\n               (bind! {:identity (str \"dao.space.dht:\"\n                                      (subs (::dht/id composition) 0 16))\n                       :deposit traffic\n                       :bind-host bind-host\n                       :bind-port bind-port\n                       :max-bytes (::dht/max-datagram dht/defaults)}))\n        ;; the DHT handle's put checks (address, oversize) over :local; its\n        ;; own replicate request goes to a sink, since the node's step\n        ;; owns every request (yin.vm.linker.dht.md 5.5)\n        front (dht/store-handle\n                {:local local\n                 :requests (ring 1)\n                 :max-message-bytes message-bound})\n        closed? (volatile! false)]\n    {:peers (vec peers)\n     :publish? publish?\n     :message-bound message-bound\n     :limits (select-keys opts [:max-backlog :repair-batch :max-open\n                                :repair-slots :repair-ticks :repair-max-ticks\n                                :max-repairing])\n     :composition composition\n     :dht (when-not socket? (dht/state composition))\n     :seam seam\n     :traffic-cursor (when socket? (oldest traffic))\n     :facts (oldest facts)\n     :ledger ledger\n     :ledger-cursor (oldest ledger)\n     :origin nil\n     :reading nil\n     :window (new-ledger)\n     :window-lost? false\n(defn announce!\n  \"Declare that the blobs put through `store` since the last announcement\n   are the publication whose manifest is `manifest-address`: `step`\n   reports it `:published` once every blob has an outcome.\"\n  [node manifest-address]\n  (stream/append! (:ledger node) {::manifest manifest-address})\n  node)\n\n\n(defn close!\n  \"Close the node's socket and its local store, discarding its ledgers\n   and queues unreported.  Idempotent; answers nil.\"\n  [node]\n  (jing/close! (:handle node))\n  nil)\n\n\n;; =============================================================================\n;; step: ticks, binding, the DHT\n;; =============================================================================\n\n(defn- tick\n  [node now]\n  (let [origin (or (:origin node) now)\n        reading (max (or (:reading node) 0) (- now origin))]\n    (stream/append! (get-in node [:composition :ticks])\n                    {:dao.lease/event :dao.lease/tick\n                     :dao.lease/reading reading})\n    (assoc node :origin origin :reading reading)))\n\n\n(defn- bind-step\n  \"While the socket binds, read its lifecycle facts: bound completes the\n   composition with the writer over the address actually bound; a failed\n   bind refuses the node.\"\n  [node]\n  (if (or (:dht node) (:refusal node))\n    [node []]\n    (let [traffic (get-in node [:composition :traffic])]\n      (loop [cursor (:traffic-cursor node)]\n        (let [next (stream/next traffic cursor)\n              v (:dao.stream/value next)]\n          (case (:dao.stream/outcome next)\n            :dao.stream/ok\n            (case (:dao.stream.datagram/event v)\n              :dao.stream.datagram/bound\n              (let [{host :dao.stream.datagram/host\n                     port :dao.stream.datagram/port}\n                    (:dao.stream.datagram/local v)\n                    descriptor {:dao.stream/type datagram/transport-type\n                                :dao.stream/identity\n                                (:dao.stream.datagram/socket v)\n                                :dao.stream.datagram/bind-host host\n                                :dao.stream.datagram/bind-port port}\n                    composition (assoc (:composition node)\n                                       :datagrams\n                                       (datagram/writer (:seam node) descriptor\n                                                        (::dht/max-datagram\n                                                          dht/defaults)))]\n                [(assoc node\n                        :composition composition\n                        :dht (dht/state composition)\n                        :traffic-cursor nil)\n                 [{::event :bound :host host :port port :id (node-id node)}]])\n\n              :dao.stream.datagram/bind-failed\n              (let [reason (str (:dao.stream.datagram/reason v))]\n                [(assoc node :refusal (str \"the socket could not bind \"\n                                           (get-in node [:composition\n                                                         ::dht/bind-host])\n                                           \": \" reason))\n                 [{::event :bind-failed :reason reason}]])\n\n              (recur (:dao.stream/cursor next)))\n\n            :dao.stream/gap (recur (:dao.stream/cursor next))\n            [(assoc node :traffic-cursor cursor) []]))))))\n\n\n(defn- read-all\n  \"Every value on `handle` after `cursor`: `[values cursor' gap?]`.\"\n(defn step\n  \"Advance the node once at the owner's millisecond reading `now`:\n   append it as a tick; finish binding; admit repairs, then the puts and\n   announcements on the ledger ring; release replicate requests; step\n   the DHT; apply its facts; advance loads; and report publications.\n   Answers `[node' events]`.  A refused node (its socket could not bind)\n   is answered unchanged.\"\n  [node now]\n  (if (:refusal node)\n    [node []]\n    (let [node (tick node now)\n          [node bound] (bind-step node)\n          node (admit-repairs node)\n          [node unknown] (admit-ledger node)\n          node (release node)\n          node (cond-> node (:dht node) (update :dht dht/step step-budget))\n          [node lost] (read-facts node)\n          [node loaded] (advance-loads node)\n          [node reported] (settle-publications node)]\n      [node (-> bound (into unknown) (into lost) (into loaded) (into\n                                                                 reported))])))\n\n\n(defn refusal\n(ns yin.shell.store\n  \"The composition's store-selection factory\n   (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md sections 3 and 6).\n\n   The engine's own selection (`yin.repl.store`) knows two stores —\n   `mem` and `file:<dir>` — and answers a generic `dao.jing` content\n   handle for each.  Every storage implementation past those two is a\n   composition's: it owns the scheme, the checked spec, the open, and the\n   transport state the open produces.  This namespace is that factory for\n   the shell compositions, so a host entry point resolves one\n   `--index-store` value against one table instead of asking the engine\n   about a backend the engine must never name.\n\n   It owns no socket, atom, promise, callback, clock or namespace global:\n   selection is a pure dispatch, and the state an open produces is\n   answered as two separate values — the engine's handle and the\n   composition's node — never as one handle with a node inside it.\"\n  (:require [yin.repl.store :as store]\n            [yin.shell.dht :as shell.dht]\n            [yin.shell.query :as shell.query]\n            [yin.shell.session :as session]))\n\n\n(def dht-scheme\n  \"The store scheme the DHT composition registers with the engine's\n   parser, as plain data: a prefix to match, a label for the refusal and\n   help text, and a directory-to-spec projection.  The engine matches the\n   prefix and calls `:parse`; it never learns what the answer means.\"\n  {:prefix shell.dht/dht-scheme\n   :label (str shell.dht/dht-scheme \"<dir>\")\n   :type :dht\n   :parse (fn [dir] {:type :dht :dir dir})})\n\n\n(def schemes\n  \"Every scheme this factory adds to the engine's own two, in the order\n   the parser tries them.\"\n  [dht-scheme])\n\n\n(defn parse-arg\n  \"The `--index-store` value as a spec, over the engine's two schemes and\n   `schemes`: `:mem`, `{:type :file :dir dir}`, or `{:type :dht :dir\n   dir}`.  A missing value, an unknown scheme and an empty directory are\n   refused with every supported form named, so the refusal an operator\n   reads lists the composition's stores too.\"\n  [value]\n  (store/parse-arg value schemes))\n\n\n(defn composition-spec?\n  \"True when `spec` names a store a composition opens rather than one the\n   engine's own selection does.\"\n  [spec]\n  (boolean (and (map? spec)\n                (some #(= (:type %) (:type spec)) schemes))))\n\n\n(defn checked-spec\n  \"Validate an `:index-store-spec` over both selections: the engine's own\n   `:mem` and `{:type :file :dir dir}`, and each registered composition's\n   own checked spec.  Anything else is refused with its reason.\"\n  [spec]\n  (if (composition-spec? spec)\n    (shell.dht/checked-spec spec)\n    (store/checked-spec spec)))\n\n\n(defn open\n  \"Open the store `spec` names, answering the two values of section 3's\n   ownership split:\n\n   ```clojure\n   {:index-store <the generic dao.jing handle the engine is constructed\n                  with>\n    :node <the composition's transport step-owner value, or nil for a\n           store with no transport>\n    :recovery <what the open validated, or nil>\n    :publication-policy <the immutable generic policy of section 5, or nil>\n    :retain-index-on-reset? bool}\n   ```\n\n   `opts` reaches the composition that opens.  They are either its own\n   names (`yin.shell.dht/open`'s `:key`, `:ws`, `:write-heads!`,\n   `:open-store`) or the host driver's neutral ones (`:publisher-key`,\n   `:ws-host`), translated here, so an engine entry point never learns what\n   a composition calls its own inputs.  `:principals`, `:admission` and\n   `:name-env` join the prepared `:link-source` of `:engine-inputs`: the\n   further `yin.repl/create-state` options this composition injects, so a\n   `(reset)` or a `(vm ...)` rebuild reconstructs a session that still\n   resolves signed names and still serves the composition's modules.\n   `:session` is the transport half of the composition session\n   (`yin.shell.session/transport-state`), which a host carries beside its\n   engine value and never inside it.\n\n   A refusal releases whatever the failed open acquired, exactly once, and\n   propagates as itself.\n\n   The engine is handed `:index-store` and never `:node`; the composition\n   keeps the node and steps it\n   (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md sections 3 and 6).\"\n  ([spec] (open spec {}))\n  ([spec opts]\n   (if (composition-spec? spec)\n     (let [_ (when (or (:content-store opts) (:content-client opts))\n               (throw (ex-info \"A composed store cannot use a second content source\"\n                               {:index-store-spec spec})))\n           key (or (:key opts) (:publisher-key opts))\n           opened (shell.dht/open spec {:key key\n                                        :ws (or (:ws opts) (:ws-host opts))\n                                        :write-heads! (:write-heads! opts)\n                                        :open-store (:open-store opts)})]\n       (assoc opened\n              :engine-inputs\n              {:link-source (shell.dht/link-source\n                              spec\n                              {:key key\n                               :principals (:principals opts)\n                               :admission (get opts :admission {})\n                               :name-env (:name-env opts)})\n               :host-modules shell.query/registrations}\n              :session (session/transport-state (:node opened) key)))\n     (let [handle (store/open (store/checked-spec spec))]\n       {:index-store handle\n        :node nil\n        :recovery (:recovery handle)\n        :publication-policy (:publication-policy handle)\n        :retain-index-on-reset? (store/retain-on-reset? handle)\n        :engine-inputs nil\n        :session nil}))))\n\n\n(defn durable?\n  \"True when `store` is a durable directory store the engine's own\n   selection opened (yin.repl.store/durable?).\"\n  [store]\n  (store/durable? store))\n\n\n(defn retain-on-reset?\n  \"True when a session rebuild continues `store`'s published index rather\n   than starting an empty one: the store's declared policy, or the\n   durable directory's own fact when it declares none\n   (yin.repl.store/retain-on-reset?).\"\n  [store]\n  (store/retain-on-reset? store))\n\n\n(defn close!\n  \"Release `store`'s lifecycle resources.  Idempotent, and the one place a\n   composition's resource owner closes the store it opened\n   (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md sections 3 and 6).\"\n  [store]\n  (store/close! store))\n(ns yin.shell.main\n  \"The outer host entry point: the engine's driver plus this project's\n   storage composition.\n\n   `yin.repl.main` is the engine's host driver — cadence, one step owner,\n   the line producers and the bounded shutdown drain — and it names no\n   transport, no peer, no key file and no store past `mem` and `file:<dir>`.\n   Everything past that reaches it as one plain-data value, `composition`,\n   assembled here out of the composition's own namespaces: the command line\n   (`yin.shell.cli`), the store factory (`yin.shell.store`), the session\n   where the section 7 adapters meet the engine (`yin.shell.session`), and\n   the DHT composition's transport lifecycle (`yin.shell.dht`).\n\n   This is the namespace the shipped binaries run — `deps.edn`'s\n   `:clj-yin-repl` and `:jolt-yin-repl`, shadow-cljs's `:yin-repl` build and\n   the ClojureDart `:main` — and it is why the engine's own dependency\n   closure can stay free of a storage implementation\n   (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md sections 2, 6 and 10, Gate A):\n   the require runs this way, and never the other way round.  `yin.shell.cli`\n   consumes the engine driver's primitives (`parse-int`, `plain-args`); the\n   engine driver consumes nothing of a composition's except the value handed\n   to it, so there is no cycle to break and nothing to resolve at runtime.\n\n   This namespace owns no socket, atom, promise, callback, clock or namespace\n   global of its own: `composition` is one constant map of function values,\n   and each host's `-main` is a single call into the engine driver's `main!`.\"\n  (:require [yin.repl.main :as repl.main]\n            [yin.shell.cli :as cli]\n            [yin.shell.dht :as shell.dht]\n            [yin.shell.session :as shell.session]\n            [yin.shell.store :as shell.store]))\n\n\n(def composition\n  \"The plain-data bundle this project's hosts hand `yin.repl.main` as its\n   `:composition`: every key that driver documents, and nothing that reaches\n   past it.\n\n   The command-line half is `yin.shell.cli/hooks` — the flags, the\n   subcommands, the key file, the help and the banner text.  The rest is the\n   store and its transport lifecycle, each entry one function value taken\n   statically from the namespace that owns it, so the wiring is identical on\n   the JVM, on Node and on Dart and no host resolves a symbol at runtime\n   (docs/design/yin.repl.dht-composition.md sections 6 and 7).\n\n   The lifecycle functions all take the one composition session\n   `{:engine … :node … :key …}` and are driven by the engine driver's single\n   tick: `:step` advances the transport and answers its lines and its own\n   events, `:recheck-on-tick` serves the link and call media the engine\n   parks on and translates those events before they cross inward, and\n   `:stop!`/`:stopped?`/`:close!` are the one resource owner's exit — asked\n   once, completed by `:step`, and forced only by the drain's last resort.\"\n  (merge (cli/hooks)\n         {:composition-spec? shell.store/composition-spec?\n          :open shell.store/open\n          :close-store! shell.store/close!\n          :step shell.dht/step\n          :recheck-on-tick shell.session/recheck-on-tick\n          :recheck-after-input shell.session/recheck-after-input\n          :stop! shell.dht/stop!\n          :stopped? shell.dht/stopped?\n          :close! shell.dht/close!\n          :busy? shell.dht/busy?\n          :refusal shell.dht/refusal\n          :admitting? shell.dht/admitting?\n          :startup-lines shell.dht/follow-lines}))\n\n\n#?(:cljd nil\n   :jolt nil\n   :clj\n   (defn -main\n     \"The shipped JVM entry point: the engine's driver over `composition`.\"\n     [& args]\n     (repl.main/main! args composition)))\n\n\n#?(:jolt\n   (defn -main\n     \"The shipped jolt entry point: the engine's driver over `composition`.\"\n     [& args]\n     (repl.main/main! args composition)))\n\n\n#?(:cljs\n   (defn -main\n     \"The shipped Node entry point: the engine's driver over `composition`.\"\n     [& args]\n     (repl.main/main! args composition)))\n\n\n#?(:cljd\n   (do\n     (defn -main\n       \"The shipped Dart entry point: the engine's driver over `composition`.\"\n       [& args]\n       (repl.main/main! args composition))\n\n     (defn ^{:dart/name main} run-main\n       [args]\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
