{"type":"item.completed","item":{"id":"item_22","type":"command_execution","command":"/bin/bash -lc 'cat collab/jolt/1792429967000-engineer-jolt-j0b-udp-probe-r2.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md && cat collab/jolt/1792485587000-engineer-jolt-j0b-linux-port.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md && cat collab/jolt/1792487422000-reviewer-jolt-j0b-linux.qwen3.8-max.findings.md && git show --stat a6c25106'","aggregated_output":"Created-GMT: 2026-10-19 17:12:47 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 00:12:47 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude\nSession-ID: 8f45af4f-0557-4cfa-a375-78525ee6a40e\n# Findings: J0b UDP probe, round 2 (review fixes R1-R3, notes R4-R6)\nRole: Stream & Network Engineer\nImplementers:\n- Model: claude-opus-5-5 | Assigned: 2026-10-20 00:12:47 +07 | Status: done (macOS arm64 record) | Rationale: resume the author to fix accepted review findings\n- Status-Event: none\n\nThis report supersedes the evidence citations of the round-1 report\n(`collab/1792429249000-engineer-jolt-j0b-udp-probe.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md`, left unedited).\nEvery number below is cited as `run-N.log:line`. The logs are under `build/jolt-spike/j0b/`.\n\n## Review findings -> what changed\n\n| finding | change |\n|---|---|\n| R1: two checks passed a literal `true` | Those two are now `RECORD` lines that cannot count as PASS, plus a third record for restoring SO_SNDBUF. The summary now separates the two: `SUMMARY 36 assertions: 36 pass, 0 fail; 3 records` (run-4/5/6.log:46). |\n| R2: round-1 report cited ports, a trace and a latency found in no log | Cause: I took them from an exploratory run before I started saving logs. This report cites only run-4..6.log by file and line. The round-1 numbers (57132/52555, trace 6761..6886, \"0.04 ms\") are withdrawn. |\n| R2 (MSG_TRUNC line) | Rechecked: in this SDK MSG_TRUNC is `sys/socket.h:574` (`grep -n MSG_TRUNC $(xcrun --show-sdk-path)/usr/include/sys/socket.h` gives `574:#define MSG_TRUNC 0x10`). The round-1 cite of 574 was correct; the reviewer's 576 does not match this SDK. MSG_TRUNC is still unused. |\n| R3: SO_SNDBUF never read back | Both setsockopt results are now recorded and read back with `get-int-option`. Requesting 2048 gives `:setsockopt [0 35], :so-sndbuf-read-back 2048` (run-4.log:31). Restoring gives `[0 35]` and a read-back of 9216 (run-4.log:33). The errno slot (35) is meaningless when the result is 0. Effect on item 3: see below. |\n| R4 (info): drain loops could hang on a persistent `:error` | New `drain!` stops on `:error` and emits a FAIL. It is used by the EMSGSIZE bisection drain and the item-3 drain. |\n| R5 (info): larger N was hardcoded | New assertion `(< 1200 big max-ok)`: `{:N 1200, :larger-N 8192, :max-sendable 9216}` PASS (run-4.log:8). |\n| R6 (info): jolt `c-bind` declares socklen_t as `:int` | Not changed: it lives in the jolt checkout, which is out of scope. Same width and ABI here, so benign. Carried to J7. |\n| Reviewer could not verify SHA, exit code or sysctls from the logs | `run.sh` now writes them into each log: jolt SHA plus a dirty-file count (line 1), Chez version (2), uname/macOS (3), sysctls (4), exit code (47). |\n\n## Verdict (unchanged in substance)\n\n**macOS arm64: PASS on all five §8.3 items.** Item 3's send-side would-block is covered by injection only. **The Linux x86_64 record (shiva) is NOT RUN, so the J0b gate stays incomplete.**\n\n| §8.3 item | macOS arm64 | Linux x86_64 |\n|---|---|---|\n| 1 | PASS (short send: injected only) | NOT RUN |\n| 2 | PASS | NOT RUN |\n| 3 | PASS by injection. Natural send would-block NOT observed (recorded, not asserted) | NOT RUN |\n| 4 | PASS | NOT RUN |\n| 5 | PASS (records in the logs) | NOT RUN |\n\n## Runs\n\n- Command, foreground from the repo root: `build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh > build/jolt-spike/j0b/run-N.log 2>&1`, for N = 4, 5, 6.\n- All three runs: exit 0 and `SUMMARY 36 assertions: 36 pass, 0 fail; 3 records` (run-4/5/6.log:46-47).\n- run-1..3.log are kept. They come from the round-1 probe, which had 37 rows including the two vacuous ones.\n- **Environment, identical in all three logs (run-4.log:1-5):**\n  - jolt `96d7b8662d7c491722445bf976b2e25e36ce5d63` with dirty=0\n  - Chez 10.4.1 (mise path)\n  - Darwin arm64, macOS 26.6.2\n  - sysctl maxdgram 9216, recvspace 786896, maxsockbuf 8388608\n  - `os.arch= aarch64`\n\n## Evidence (run-4.log unless noted)\n\n**Item 1.**\n- **Bind and readback:** ephemeral ports a=63735 and b=52188 were read back (run-4.log:9). The other runs got 51232/55849 (run-5.log:9) and 57318/63271 (run-6.log:9).\n- **Empty socket:** `:would-block` errno 35 (:10).\n- **N=1200 round trips** (`:from {:ip \"127.0.0.1\" :port 63735}` on each):\n  - zero-length (:11)\n  - embedded-NUL, 8 bytes (:12)\n  - 1200 bytes (:13)\n- **Consecutive packets:** lens `[10 11 12 13 14]` were received as `[10 11 12 13 14]`, then `:would-block` (:14).\n- **Short send [injected]:** `{:result :short-send, :n 99, :len 100}`, and the receiver got nothing (:15).\n- **N=8192:** the same rows again at :21-26, with an 8192-byte packet at :24.\n\n**Item 2.**\n- **Platform maximum:** the largest sendable packet is 9216. 9217 gave `{:result :emsgsize, :errno 40}` (:6), with `:so-sndbuf 9216, :so-rcvbuf 786896`.\n- **Larger N below the maximum:** asserted (:8).\n- **N=1200:**\n  - 1200 bytes gave `:packet` (:16).\n  - 1201 gave `:oversize :received 1201` (:17).\n  - 1300 gave `:oversize :received 1201` (:18).\n  - Queue empty afterwards (:19).\n- **N=8192:** same pattern at :27-30.\n- Note: run-6.log:29 shows `:waits 0`, meaning the packet was already queued, so no wait was needed.\n\n**Item 3.**\n- **RECORD [natural]:** SO_SNDBUF was requested at 2048 and read back as 2048 (macOS did not round it). 20000 sends of 1024 bytes gave `{{:result :sent} 20000}` and `:would-block-observed? false` (:31). The receiver then drained 745 packets (:32). The same 745 appears in run-5.log:32 and run-6.log:32.\n- **How SO_SNDBUF affects the observation:**\n  - Verified: shrinking SO_SNDBUF to 2048, confirmed by read-back, did not induce would-block, and 1024-byte sends still succeeded.\n  - Inference, not proven by this probe: on macOS a UDP socket keeps no send queue against SO_SNDBUF on loopback. SO_SNDBUF acts as the per-datagram send limit (the default equals maxdgram 9216, and EMSGSIZE fires above it, :6). The packet goes straight to the receiver, where the overflow is dropped silently. 745 x 1024 = 762880 bytes, which is under recvspace 786896, consistent with receive-buffer drops.\n  - So a smaller SO_SNDBUF cannot make loopback produce EAGAIN/ENOBUFS here. Natural send would-block stays unobserved, and the requirement rests on injection.\n- **[injected]:**\n  - `[-1 35]` gave `{:result :would-block, :errno 35}` (:34).\n  - `[-1 55]` gave `{:result :would-block, :errno 55}` (:35).\n  - No packet was accepted in either case.\n- **[natural]:** a real 1024-byte send and receive succeeded after the injections (:36). A receive on an empty socket gave `:would-block` errno 35 (:37).\n\n**Item 4.**\n- **Parking and wakeup:** trace `[[6769 :waiter-start] [6917 [:worker-ticked 50 :waiter-state :parked]] [6917 [:worker-sends {:result :sent, :n 77}]] [6917 [:waiter-got :packet 1]]]` (:38). The waiter was `:parked` while the worker fiber ran 50 ticks, and the 77-byte packet woke it within the same millisecond. Timestamps are `poller/clock-ms`.\n  - run-5.log:38 trace: 6642/6789.\n  - run-6.log:38 trace: 6684/6830.\n- **Cancellation:**\n  - `cancel!` released a `:parked` waiter, the join returned `:released`, and the retry was `:would-block` (:39).\n  - A wait on the cancelled fd returned in `11/250` ms = 0.044 ms (:40). Other runs: `3/125` = 0.024 ms (run-5.log:40) and `29/500` = 0.058 ms (run-6.log:40).\n- **Close:**\n  - Close after `forget!` returned 0 (:41).\n  - A `recvfrom` on the closed fd gave `{:result :error, :errno 9}` (:42).\n  - The closed fd is absent from the poller table (:43). The `fds {6 …}` entry shown there is the still-open probe socket b's ready tombstone, not the closed fd.\n\n**Item 5, cleanup.**\n- Both sockets closed with result 0 (:44).\n- The final `debug-state` was `{:pending {}, … :fds {}}` (:45).\n\n## Not covered (unchanged)\n- Linux x86_64 on shiva: NOT RUN.\n- IPv6.\n- Natural send-side EAGAIN/ENOBUFS.\n- recvmsg/MSG_TRUNC: not bound; truncation is detected with the N+1 sentinel.\n- Datagram/DHT conformance and the J7 writer mapping.\n\n## Files (round 2)\n- Modified: `build/jolt-spike/j0b/udp_probe.clj` (record/drain!/SO_SNDBUF read-back/N assertion/summary) and `build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh` (environment header and exit line).\n- Created: `build/jolt-spike/j0b/run-4.log`, `run-5.log`, `run-6.log`, and this report.\n\nNothing outside `build/jolt-spike/j0b/` and this report was touched. Nothing was staged or committed.\nCreated-GMT: 2026-10-20 08:39:47 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 15:39:47 +07\nCoding-Agent: claude\nSession-ID: 8f45af4f-0557-4cfa-a375-78525ee6a40e\n# Findings: J0b UDP probe made platform-aware (macOS arm64 and Linux x86_64)\nRole: Stream & Network Engineer\nImplementers:\n- Model: claude-opus-5-5 | Assigned: 2026-10-20 15:39:47 +07 | Status: done (macOS verified; Linux NOT RUN by me) | Rationale: owner: \"push the jolt-j0a branch and run J0b on shiva\"\n\n**macOS behaviour is unchanged:** `SUMMARY 36 assertions: 36 pass, 0 fail; 3 records`, exit 0, on 5 foreground runs. The PASS/RECORD labels are identical to `run-6.log`.\n\n**The Linux expectations below are written but UNTESTED.** I cannot run on shiva.\n\nFiles touched: only `build/jolt-spike/j0b/udp_probe.clj`, `build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh`, and new logs `run-7..11.log`. `run-1..6.log` are kept. Nothing was staged.\n\n## What changed\n\n### `udp_probe.clj`\n\n1. **Platform selection.** The platform is `jolt.socket.native/os`, the same value jolt's socket layer and sockaddr helpers branch on (`:macos`, `:linux`, `:windows`, derived inside `native.clj`). The probe never reads `os.name` itself. The constants are one table per platform:\n\n| constant | `:macos` (MacOSX.sdk, as before) | `:linux` (shiva headers, orchestrator-verified) |\n|---|---|---|\n| SOCK_DGRAM / IPPROTO_UDP | 2 / 17 | 2 / 17 |\n| SO_SNDBUF / SO_RCVBUF | 0x1001 / 0x1002 | 7 / 8 |\n| MSG_TRUNC (unused) | 0x10 | 0x20 |\n| EAGAIN = EWOULDBLOCK | 35 | 11 |\n| EMSGSIZE | 40 | 90 |\n| ENOBUFS | 55 | 105 |\n| EBADF | 9 | 9 |\n\n   Any other platform throws at load. The header line prints `platform=`, the `os.name`/`os.arch` the shim gives (possibly nil on Linux) and the whole constant table. The sockaddr layout (macOS `sin_len` byte versus Linux 16-bit family) is already handled per platform by `native/make-sockaddr` and `sockaddr-*`, so nothing in the probe changed for it.\n2. **Constants flow through everywhere:**\n   - send-error classification: EAGAIN/ENOBUFS give `:would-block`, EMSGSIZE gives `:emsgsize`;\n   - recv `:would-block`;\n   - the injected-errno cases use the platform's EAGAIN and ENOBUFS (11 and 105 on Linux), and stay labelled `[injected]`;\n   - the EBADF check after close;\n   - the label of the empty-receive check now prints the platform's errno (still reads \"errno 35\" on macOS).\n3. **The platform maximum is measured, not assumed.** The bisection over [1, 65507] was already measured, and it is unchanged:\n   - macOS stops at 9216 (`maxdgram` = SO_SNDBUF);\n   - on Linux it should reach 65507, the IPv4 UDP payload limit.\n\n   The EMSGSIZE-above-max check uses the platform's EMSGSIZE. The \"configured larger N (8192) below the observed max\" check asserts `(< 1200 8192 max-ok)` against the measured value on both platforms.\n4. **SO_SNDBUF read-back.**\n   - **macOS (unchanged):** the requested values 2048 and 9216 are read back as RECORDs only, to keep the 36/3 counts.\n   - **Linux (one extra assertion):** `[linux] SO_SNDBUF read-back follows socket(7): >= 2 x requested`. It checks that both setsockopt calls answer 0 and that the read-back values are at least 4096 and at least 18432. Linux doubles the request for bookkeeping, clamps it at `net.core.wmem_max` (212992 on shiva, above both requests) and floors it at `SOCK_MIN_SNDBUF`. I chose \"≥ 2×\" so a floor above 2×2048 still passes, never equality.\n   - The RECORD lines still show the raw values on both platforms.\n5. **Natural would-block stays a RECORD** on both platforms (20000 × 1024-byte sends with no reader, histogram plus delivered count). Whatever Linux does is recorded, not asserted.\n6. **Comments:** sendto all-or-nothing now says (macOS, Linux); socklen_t is 32-bit unsigned on both.\n\n**Expected Linux totals: 37 assertions, 3 records** (the one extra Linux check).\n\n### `run.sh` (POSIX sh; checked with `sh -n` and run under `/bin/dash`)\n\n- **jolt binary:** `$JOLT`, else `build/tools/jolt/bin/jolt` (J0a layout), else `build/jolt-spike/jolt/bin/jolt` (spike layout). The probe path comes from the script's own directory, so it can be run from any checkout root.\n- **`JOLT_CHEZ`:**\n  1. env;\n  2. else source `build/tools/jolt-env.sh` when present (J0a layout);\n  3. else the mise 10.4.1 path if it exists;\n  4. else unset, and jolt searches PATH (a line says so).\n- **Prints:** the jolt path, jolt SHA and dirty count (git -C the jolt home), and the Chez version. Then, per OS:\n  - **Darwin:** `uname -sm`, `sw_vers`, sysctls `net.inet.udp.maxdgram`, `net.inet.udp.recvspace`, `kern.ipc.maxsockbuf`, and the lo0 MTU.\n  - **Linux:** `uname -sm`, the `/etc/os-release` PRETTY_NAME, `/proc/sys/net/core/{wmem_default,wmem_max,rmem_default,rmem_max}`, `/proc/sys/net/ipv4/{udp_wmem_min,udp_rmem_min,udp_mem}`, and `/sys/class/net/lo/mtu`.\n\n  No macOS-only command runs on Linux, and every probe of the environment tolerates a missing file.\n- **Ends with** `exit: N`.\n\n## macOS evidence (foreground, this Mac, jolt 96d7b866, Chez 10.4.1)\n\n| run | how | result |\n|---|---|---|\n| `run-7.log`, `run-8.log`, `run-9.log` | `build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh` from the repo root (spike layout default) | exit 0, `SUMMARY 36 assertions: 36 pass, 0 fail; 3 records` |\n| `run-10-dash-j0a.log` | `JOLT=…/jolt-j0a/build/tools/jolt/bin/jolt dash build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh` | exit 0, 36/0/3 |\n| `run-11-j0a-default.log` | from the jolt-j0a worktree root with `JOLT`/`JOLT_CHEZ` unset (exercises the `build/tools/jolt` + `jolt-env.sh` default; that worktree stayed clean) | exit 0, 36/0/3 |\n\n- **Header** (`run-7.log`):\n  - `platform= :macos` with constants `{:so-sndbuf 4097 … :eagain 35 :emsgsize 40 :enobufs 55 :ebadf 9}` (0x1001 = 4097);\n  - sysctls `9216 786896 8388608`, lo0 MTU 16384;\n  - max sendable 9216, EMSGSIZE errno 40.\n- **Labels:** the PASS/FAIL/RECORD label sequence of `run-7.log` is identical to `run-6.log` (diff empty).\n\n## Linux x86_64 expectations, all UNTESTED (for the shiva run)\n\n1. **Platform and constants:**\n   - `native/os` answers `:linux` and the header shows the Linux constant table. If the shim's nil `os.name` made `native/os` fall to `:linux` on shiva, that is the right answer there.\n   - Bind, readback and sockaddr decoding work through the Linux sockaddr layout. This is jolt's code path and is unexercised by me.\n2. **Packet semantics (item 1):** the same assertions as macOS, with EAGAIN 11 on the empty receive.\n3. **Platform maximum (item 2):**\n   - The bisection should find **65507**, and 65508 should give EMSGSIZE errno **90** with nothing delivered.\n   - Big sends land in a 212992-byte receive buffer, which may drop some of the bisection's packets. The drain counts them; that is not asserted.\n   - Item 2 for N=1200 and N=8192 should pass unchanged (8192 < 65507).\n4. **SO_SNDBUF:** the read-back for 2048 is expected around 4608 (the doubled request, possibly floored at SOCK_MIN_SNDBUF), and 18432 for 9216. The `[linux]` assertion requires only ≥ 2× the request.\n5. **Natural would-block (RECORD):** Linux may or may not produce EAGAIN/ENOBUFS when blasting with a small SO_SNDBUF. Loopback frees the send buffer on delivery, so I expect `{:sent 20000}` again with silent receiver drops, but it is unknown.\n6. **Injected cases:** EAGAIN 11 and ENOBUFS 105 should give `:would-block` with no packet accepted.\n7. **Poller and fibers (item 4):** this runs on jolt's epoll backend, not kqueue. The parked waiter, wakeup, `cancel!`, `forget!`, close then EBADF 9, and the empty poller table are all unexercised on Linux by me.\n8. **`run.sh` on dash/Ubuntu:** the `/proc` and `/sys` reads and `/etc/os-release` parsing are untested on Linux. They were only shown not to run on Darwin. The script did run under `/bin/dash` on macOS.\n9. **Toolchain on shiva:** the J0a provisioning of Chez 10.4.1 and the jolt checkout is a precondition. `run.sh` uses `build/tools/jolt-env.sh` if bootstrap has run there.\n\n**Expected Linux total: 37 assertions + 3 records.** Any FAIL there is a real finding to report, not something to adjust the probe to.\n\nReport: `/Users/sto/workspace/datomworld/collab/1792485587000-engineer-jolt-j0b-linux-port.claude-opus-5-5.findings.md`\nCreated-GMT: 2026-10-20 09:10:22 GMT\nCreated-Local: 2026-10-20 16:10:22 +07\nCoding-Agent: cmd\nSession-ID: 61966f9d-4875-4b42-9545-b0dc8a2c9891\n# Review: J0b platform-aware probe + Linux x86_64 evidence\nRole: Reviewer (read-only) | Model: qwen/qwen3.8-max\n\n## Verdict: SIGNED OFF\n\n## Findings\n\n| id | sev | file:line | evidence | correction |\n|---|---|---|---|---|\n| F1 | info | build/jolt-spike/j0b/run.sh:12-24 | `JOLT` default and the `jolt-env.sh` source are CWD-relative (probe path is `$0`-relative); must run from the repo root. Header says so; run-11 exercised the J0a layout. | None required; optionally resolve both from `$dir`. |\n| F2 | info (record) | udp_probe.clj:249-255, run-7.log/shiva-1.log RECORD lines | Natural send-side would-block never observed on either platform (histogram `{:sent 20000}` both); the `:would-block` send path and short-send rest on `[injected]` seams. §8.3:954-956 explicitly permits this when loopback cannot induce it, labelled separately, with real successful syscalls also exercised - all three conditions met (\"[natural] real sendto/recvfrom succeed after injection\"; natural receive-side EAGAIN asserted, errno 35/11). | Keep the RECORD in the J0b gate record; a real full-send-buffer induction remains unobserved on both platforms. |\n\n## Checks\n\n- (a) Constants all correct: Linux EAGAIN=EWOULDBLOCK 11, EMSGSIZE 90, ENOBUFS 105, EBADF 9, SO_SNDBUF 7, SO_RCVBUF 8, MSG_TRUNC 0x20, SOCK_DGRAM 2, IPPROTO_UDP 17; macOS 35/40/55/9, 0x1001/0x1002, 0x10, 2, 17. One table per platform via `native/os` (udp_probe.clj:21-29); any other platform throws at load. No cross-contamination: labels, injection errnos and classification all flow from `consts` (shiva log shows errno 11/105 labels, macOS 35/55).\n- (b) The `[linux]` check (udp_probe.clj:265-269) is not a tautology: it requires both setsockopt rc 0 and real read-backs ≥ 2× request. Observed 4608/18432: 4608 is the SOCK_MIN_SNDBUF floor (above 2×2048=4096), 18432 exactly 2×9216 - matching socket(7) doubling/clamp/floor semantics; a non-doubling kernel would read 2048 and fail. `>=` (never equality) is the right tolerance for the floor; honestly labelled and Linux-only, keeping macOS at 36/3.\n- (c) macOS 36 labels are an exact subset of Linux's 37 (only the `[linux]` check added); errno numerals in labels track platform constants. Nothing weakened or skipped on Linux: items 1, 2, 4, 5 fully natural on both (bisection measured 9216 vs 65507, EMSGSIZE 40/90 asserted with nothing delivered, oversize never yields a prefix and is consumed whole, MSG_TRUNC declared-unused per the N+1 sentinel, N=1200/8192 below both maxima, fiber park/wake/cancel/close/EBADF/poller-empty on epoll, cleanup counts). Item 3 is the injection-dependent one - see F2.\n- (d) Report claims match the logs: it was written pre-shiva with Linux expectations flagged UNTESTED and predicted 37/3; shiva-1..3 each show exactly `37 assertions: 37 pass, 0 fail; 3 records`, exit 0, identical headers (jolt 96d7b866 dirty=0, Chez 10.4.1 threaded, Ubuntu 24.04.5). J0a precondition on shiva evidenced (bootstrap: pins verified, cmake 4.4.4 linux-x86_64; smoke `SUMMARY ok`). Item 5 records complete: SHAs, arch, sysctls/proc values, MTU, lengths/counts, error traces, wakeup traces, cleanup.\n- (e) run.sh is POSIX sh; macOS-only commands (`sw_vers`, `sysctl`, `ifconfig`) sit strictly in the Darwin case arm; the Linux arm reads `/proc/sys`, `/sys/class/net/lo/mtu`, `/etc/os-release` with missing-file tolerance. Proven by execution: dash on macOS and three real Ubuntu runs.\n\n## Could not verify\n\n- Probe byte-identity local↔shiva (orchestrator's sha256, trusted); shiva-2/3 full label diffs (headers/summaries grepped, identical).\n- Linux constant values against shiva's actual headers (matched against my knowledge of asm-generic/x86_64 values, not read from the machine).\n- jolt epoll-poller internals beyond the logged traces; the run-6↔run-7 label diff claim.\ncommit a6c25106eefde839ca5897d8e7febc4b82a4a3fa\nAuthor: Sonny To <son.c.to@gmail.com>\nDate:   Thu Nov 5 12:09:43 2026 +0700\n\n    fix(yang.clojure): the jolt-conditional require in its loadable form — top-level conditional, aliases intact\n    \n    The first cut (a list-of-libspecs under :default inside ns :require)\n    was kondo-unparsable and possibly JVM-unloadable; neither [] nor nil\n    works as an empty :require libspec on the JVM. The repo-safe form: the\n    ns :require stays clean, and a top-level #?(:jolt nil :default (require\n    '... :as treader] ...)) loads the mvn reader on every non-jolt host —\n    verified on the JVM (load OK; read-forms reads 2 forms), kondo 0/0.\n\n src/cljc/yang/clojure.cljc | 14 ++++++++++----\n 1 file changed, 10 insertions(+), 4 deletions(-)\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
