ts 4300})))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:88:  (runners-under (opts-under {::integer/max-bits 60,\ntest/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:89:                              ::integer/max-digits 5})))\ntest/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:1115:      (is (= [::integer/digit-limit\ntest/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:1134:;; 2^61 - 1: each is a module `::bit-limit`, which the prelude raises as\ntest/yang/python/antlr/prelude_parity_test.cljc:1135:;; MemoryError; decimal text past 5 digits is `::digit-limit`, raised as\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/linked_prelude_test.cljc:112:                        (data/register-data-module {::data/max-items 1048576})\ntest/yang/python/antlr/float_address_test.cljc:301:                (data/register-data-module {::data/max-items 1048576})\ntest/yang/python/antlr/float_address_test.cljc:303:                  {::integer/max-bits 100000, ::integer/max-digits 4300})\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_module_test.cljc:20:      (data/register-data-module {::data/max-items 1048576})\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_module_test.cljc:21:      (prelude/register-integer-module {::integer/max-bits 100000,\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/prelude_module_test.cljc:22:                                        ::integer/max-digits 4300})\ntest/yang/clojure_test.clj:715:                              {::yang/convention\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/lower_test.clj:493:                    (vec (take-while #(not= ::eof %)\ntest/yang/typescript/antlr/lower_test.clj:494:                                     (repeatedly #(read {:eof ::eof} r))))))]\ntest/dao/space/query_test.cljc:454:               :dao.stream/cursor {::pos 0}})\n(ns dao.stream.ws.pump\n  \"Jolt-only owner-thread pump under the native WebSocket host adapter (J6d;\n   J6 plan rulings A1-A5). One instance per `create` call: its registry of\n   descriptors, write queues, close records and deadlines live in that\n   instance and nowhere else, so two instances never share state.\n\n   Ownership (A1). Every entry runs on the thread that drives the instance;\n   nothing here waits, parks or polls a socket. A driver polls the `interest`\n   descriptors until the earlier of its cadence and `next-deadline`\n   (`jolt.io-poller/clock-ms`, nil when nothing is due), then hands the\n   `[[fd revents] ...]` it observed to `pump!`, `[]` on a timeout: every\n   call runs the due expiries. A composition that never pumps an instance\n   makes no progress, by design.\n\n   Bounded turn (A1). One `pump!` visits each ready descriptor at most once,\n   starting after the one visited last in the previous turn (round robin in\n   registration order). A connection visit attempts at most one write of\n   the head of its queue and reads at most one chunk (one reused 64 KiB\n   native buffer); a listener visit accepts at most `accept-batch`.\n\n   One ordered write path (A3). Each connection owns one FIFO of whole wire\n   segments; the head carries an offset and is never rewritten. `send!`\n   writes at once only into an empty FIFO and queues the unsent tail;\n   otherwise it appends. Protocol output (pongs, close frames) joins the\n   same FIFO at generation time; nothing has priority. `send!` answers nil\n   or `{:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed}`, never false.\n   `queued-bytes` counts every byte handed over and not yet accepted by the\n   kernel, control frames included; 0 after the terminal.\n\n   Two connection modes. A :raw connection (an HTTP upgrade in progress, or\n   a plain byte stream) delivers `:on-data` chunks, `:on-eof` once and\n   `:on-error` once. `upgraded!` turns it into a :ws connection, which parses\n   its input one frame at a time through the injected `:protocol`\n   (`{:parser :parse-frame :encode-frame :close-payload :mask-key}`; the\n   parse contract is `(parse-frame state chunk offset)` =>\n   `[event-or-nil state' offset']` with `:message`, `:ping`, `:pong`,\n   `:close` and `:fail` events, a close-frame failure carrying `:close?\n   true`), answers each ping with one pong, delivers `:on-message`, and\n   reports exactly one `:on-closed` terminal.\n\n   Control budget (A4). At most `max-pongs` pending pongs per connection plus\n   one reserved close slot. When the pongs fill the budget the connection\n   stops parsing after that frame, leaves read interest and keeps the\n   unparsed rest of its chunk; when a drain frees a pong slot the rest is\n   parsed before any new read, and read interest returns once it is\n   consumed. The close slot never counts against the budget and is never\n   refused.\n\n   Close record (A5). `{:local nil|:queued|:sent, :peer nil|:received,\n   :peer-status nil|:absent|:invalid|code, :code, :reason, :initiator,\n   :deadline}`. The first of a local `close!` and the peer's close frame\n   starts the 5 s `close-deadline`; the first local request wins. The\n   terminal code: a completed handshake reports the local code when this\n   side initiated, else the peer's status (an empty close reports 1005, an\n   invalid one 1002); a peer close followed by end of stream before the\n   response left reports the peer's status; end of stream, reset or a\n   transfer failure without a peer close reports 1006; deadline expiry in\n   any state reports 1006. A parser `:fail` without `:close?` fails the\n   connection (RFC 6455 section 7.1.7) and opens no handshake: the record\n   gets `:failed? true`, the close frame takes the reserved slot, input is\n   read only to see end of stream and discarded, and once the frame is\n   written the terminal is the local code with cause `:fail`; the peer's\n   reply is never parsed. A `:fail` after a local `close!` keeps that\n   request's frame and code and ends the same way. 1005 and 1006 are never\n   put on the wire. A ping that arrives once a local close is queued gets no\n   pong: the pong would queue behind our close frame, RFC 6455 section 5.5.1\n   forbids only data frames after a close, and no Autobahn case exercises\n   it.\n\n   Listeners. Accepted sockets register with `:listener` and `:upgrading?`;\n   at `max-pending-upgrades` upgrading sockets (or `:max-connections` owned\n   ones) the listener leaves the interest set until a slot frees. An\n   upgrade not completed within its timeout is released and reported to\n   `:on-upgrade-expired`. `close-listener!` closes the listener descriptor;\n   `drained?` answers true once no connection it owns is left, and its\n   `:on-drained` is called once then. `lower-deadlines!` brings the close\n   deadline of every connection a listener owns to at most a given time.\n\n   Callbacks. Data callbacks (`:on-data`, `:on-message`) run inside the\n   visit. Terminals and other lifecycle reports run at the start or end of\n   a turn, never inside an entry the composition called; `next-deadline`\n   answers now while one is owed.\n\n   Native memory: the read and write buffers exist while the registry is\n   non-empty and are freed when its last descriptor leaves.\"\n  (:require\n    [dao.stream.ws.tcp :as tcp]\n    [jolt.ffi :as ffi]\n    [jolt.io-poller :as poller]\n    [jolt.socket.native :as native]))\n\n\n(def chunk-bytes\n  \"The read chunk and the largest single write attempt.\"\n  (* 64 1024))\n\n\n(def accept-batch\n  \"The most connections one listener visit accepts.\"\n  16)\n\n\n(def max-pongs\n  \"Pending pongs per connection before its input pauses (A4).\"\n  16)\n\n\n(def close-deadline-ms\n  \"From the first close event to forced teardown (A5).\"\n  5000)\n\n\n(def max-pending-upgrades\n  \"Default per-listener cap on accepted, not yet upgraded sockets (A4).\"\n  64)\n\n\n(def upgrade-timeout-ms\n  \"Default deadline for an upgrade, from accept or connect (A4).\"\n  10000)\n\n\n(def closed-outcome {:dao.stream/outcome :dao.stream/closed})\n\n\n   `line!` is given) and `pump`'s interest descriptors (when `pump` is\n   given) until one is ready or the earlier of `sleep-ms` and the pump's\n   `:deadline` (`jolt.io-poller/clock-ms`; nil, or a pump without one, is\n   the cadence alone) passes; then drain the wake pipe, run one\n   `(:pump! pump)` turn over the observed `[[fd revents] ...]` (every\n   round; `[]` after a timeout), then deposit stdin lines through `line!`\n   (nil at end of input). A deadline already due still polls once, with a\n   zero timeout, so readiness seen then reaches the same `pump!` turn as\n   the expiry. Never evaluates. Answers truthy when anything was woken,\n   moved or deposited.\"\n  [wake sleep-ms pump line!]\n  (let [interest (if pump (vec ((:interest pump))) [])\n        base (if line! 2 1)\n        n (+ base (count interest))\n        fds (native/alloc-pollfds n)\n        cadence (poller/deadline-ms sleep-ms)\n        due (when-let [f (:deadline pump)] (f))\n        deadline (if due (min due cadence) cadence)]\n    (try\n      (native/init-pollfd! fds 0 (:read wake) native/pollin)\n      (when line! (native/init-pollfd! fds 1 0 native/pollin))\n      (dotimes [i (count interest)]\n        (let [[fd events] (nth interest i)]\n          (native/init-pollfd! fds (+ base i) fd events)))\n      (loop []\n        (let [remaining (max 0 (long (Math/ceil (- deadline (poller/clock-ms)))))\n              [rc e] (native/c-poll fds n remaining)]\n          (cond\n            (and (neg? rc) (native/eintr? e)) (recur)\n            (neg? rc) (throw (ex-info \"owner poll failed\" {:errno e}))\n            :else nil)))\n      (let [ready (vec (keep (fn [i]\n                               (let [rev (native/pollfd-revents fds (+ base i))]\n                                 (when (pos? rev) [(first (nth interest i)) rev])))\n                             (range (count interest))))\n            woken? (drain! wake)\n            progressed? (when pump (:progressed? ((:pump! pump) ready)))\n            stdin? (when (and line! (pos? (native/pollfd-revents fds 1)))\n                     (read-stdin! wake line!))]\n        (or woken? progressed? stdin?))\n      (finally (ffi/free fds)))))\n\n\n(defn sleep-input!\n  \"Wait for a nudge, stdin, or the cadence deadline. `line!` only deposits\n   lines (nil at EOF). Never evaluate from this seam.\"\n  [wake sleep-ms line!]\n  (sleep-fds! wake sleep-ms nil line!))\n241:(def codec-profiles\n\n(defn open\n  \"Dial the URL's endpoint by the two identities and return immediately.\n   `:now` is the driver's clock reading, recorded on the dial so that a\n   connection that never opens is lost at `give-up-after`; without it\n   nothing expires.  Both reflections are attached at once -- the\n   contract's deferred remote confirmation -- so a successful result is\n   reported `:established` without waiting on any transport event.  The\n   RPC client's response cursor is the `:dao.stream/newest` anchor;\n   `rpc/poll!` resolves it, retrying while the answers reflection answers\n   a retryable mint.  `:codec` is the wire profile, CBOR when absent.\"\n  [{:keys [url host now codec] :or {codec cbor/profile}}]\n  (let [parsed (parse-url url)]\n    (cond\n      (not= :yin.repl.connect/parsed (get parsed outcome-key))\n      parsed\n\n      (not (host-common/adapter? host))\n      (failure :yin.repl.connect/no-host-adapter\n               (host-common/missing-message \"(connect …) is not wired\"))\n\n      :else\n      (let [spec (get parsed spec-key)\n            [d refusal] (dial url spec host now codec)]\n        (or refusal\n            {outcome-key :yin.repl.connect/attached\n             connection-key {:url url\n                             :spec spec\n                             :host host\n                             :codec codec\n                             :dial d\n                             :status :established\n                             :detached-by nil}\n             client-key (rpc/client-state (requests-handle d)\n                                          (answers-handle d)\n                                          stream/anchor-newest)})))))\n\n\n(defn step!\n  \"Step this connection's dial one tick at the driver's `now` and answer\n   the stepped connection: its projection, so what the wire delivered\n   reaches the reflections' channel ring, the link at `now` (expiry), then\n   this end's own mirror step (a REPL client serves nothing through it,\n   but the composition is symmetric).  The dial is a value: a caller must\n   keep the returned connection and step it every tick it polls the RPC\n   client, or the channel ring never receives what the server sent.  A\n   nil `now` expires nothing.\"\n  [connection now]\n  (cond-> connection\n    (:dial connection) (update :dial remote-channel/dial-step now)))\n\n   {:ns \"dao.stream.ws-project-jvm-test\"\n    :reason \"cross-host ws peer suite (JVM)\" :capability \"ws sockets + peer\" :owner \"J6/J7 (J2b oracle)\"}\n   {:ns \"dao.stream.udp-test\" :reason \"UDP sockets\" :capability \"udp sockets\" :owner \"J6/J7\"}\n   {:ns \"dao.stream.udp-raw-test\" :reason \"UDP sockets\" :capability \"udp sockets\" :owner \"J6/J7\"}\n   {:ns \"dao.stream.datagram.jvm-test\"\n    :reason \"JVM datagram socket seam\" :capability \"udp sockets\" :owner \"J6/J7\"}\n   {:ns \"dao.stream.datagram.node-test\"\n    :reason \"Node datagram lane (cljs)\" :capability \"udp sockets + Node\" :owner \"J6/J7\"}\n   {:ns \"dao.stream.datagram.dart-test\"\n    :reason \"Dart datagram lane (cljd)\" :capability \"udp sockets + Dart\" :owner \"J6/J7\"}\n   {:ns \"dao.jing.stream-test\"\n    :reason \"requires dao.stream.transit (missed by the round-1 manifest; found by the round-2 load evidence)\"\n    :capability \"transit codec\" :owner \"jolt seat (design 9.4 ruling)\"}\n   {:ns \"dao.jing.dht.socket-test\"\n    :reason \"binds real datagram sockets\" :capability \"udp sockets\" :owner \"J6/J7\"}\n   {:ns \"yin.vm.ffi.remote-serve-test\"\n    :reason \"the FFI remote serve suite\" :capability \"FFI-remote\" :owner \"J7\"}\n   {:ns \"yin.vm.ffi.remote-serve.responder-test\"\n    :reason \"the FFI remote responder suite\" :capability \"FFI-remote\" :owner \"J7\"}\n   {:ns \"yin.vm.ucf.stage-e-process-test\"\n    :reason \"spawns child processes\" :capability \"child processes\" :owner \"J7\"}\n   {:ns \"yin.vm.linker.cross-host-transfer-test\"\n    :reason \"cross-host transfer over dao.stream.transit + remote peers\"\n    :capability \"transit codec + peer\" :owner \"J2b + J6/J7\"}\n   {:ns \"dao.space.dht-test\"\n    :reason \"socket behaviour on jolt is unproven; deferred to J6/J7\"\n    :capability \"udp datagram host seam (java.net.DatagramSocket)\" :owner \"J6/J7\"}\n   {:ns \"yin.vm.linker.dht-test\"\n    :reason \"socket behaviour on jolt is unproven; deferred to J6/J7\"\n    :capability \"udp datagram host seam\" :owner \"J6/J7\"}\n   {:ns \"yin.vm.linker.dht-end-to-end-test\"\n    :reason \"socket behaviour on jolt is unproven; deferred to J6/J7\"\n    :capability \"udp datagram host seam + yin.repl\" :owner \"J6/J7 + J3\"}\n   {:ns \"yin.vm.linker.head-board-test\"\n    :reason \"socket behaviour on jolt is unproven; deferred to J6/J7\"\n    :capability \"udp datagram host seam\" :owner \"J6/J7\"}\n   {:ns \"yin.vm.linker.head-follow-test\"\n    :reason \"socket behaviour on jolt is unproven; deferred to J6/J7\"\n    :capability \"udp datagram host seam\" :owner \"J6/J7\"}\n   {:ns \"dao.stream.ble-test\"\n    :reason \"BLE radio transport tests (dao.stream.ble)\"\n    :capability \"BLE radio / hardware\" :owner \"BLE track\"}\n   {:ns \"dao.stream.ble.dart-test\"\n    :reason \"Dart BLE seam in flutter_test sandbox (dao.stream.ble.dart)\"\n    :capability \"BLE radio + Flutter\" :owner \"BLE track\"}\n   {:ns \"dao.stream.ble.links-test\"\n1ab4e9e65c48a437c46fce637de0dfc44f6d07ae docs(collab): the j7 review-round prompt\n1794773508 2026-11-15 20:11:48 UTC\n","exit_code":0,"status":"completed"}}
